-- # Class: OcsfCertificate Description: The Digital Certificate, also known as a Public Key Certificate, object contains information about the ownership and usage of a public key. It serves as a means to establish trust in the authenticity and integrity of the public key and the associated entity.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: created_time Description: The time when the certificate was created.
--     * Slot: created_time_dt Description: The time when the certificate was created.
--     * Slot: expiration_time Description: The expiration time of the certificate.
--     * Slot: expiration_time_dt Description: The expiration time of the certificate.
--     * Slot: is_self_signed Description: Denotes whether a digital certificate is self-signed or signed by a known certificate authority (CA).
--     * Slot: issuer Description: The certificate issuer distinguished name.
--     * Slot: serial_number Description: The serial number of the certificate used to create the digital signature.
--     * Slot: subject Description: The certificate subject distinguished name.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the certificate.
--     * Slot: version Description: The certificate version.
-- # Class: OcsfCheck Description: The check object defines a specific, testable compliance verification point that evaluates a target device against a standard, framework, or custom requirement. While checks are typically associated with formal standards (like CIS, NIST, or ISO), they can also represent custom or organizational requirements. When mapped to controls, checks can evaluate specific control_parameters to determine compliance status, but neither the control mapping nor control_parameters are required for a valid check.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: desc Description: The detailed description of the compliance check, explaining the security requirement, vulnerability, or configuration being assessed. For example, CIS: <code>The cramfs filesystem type is a compressed read-only Linux filesystem. Removing support for unneeded filesystem types reduces the local attack surface.</code> or DISA STIG: <code>Unauthorized access to the information system by foreign entities may result in loss or compromise of data.</code>
--     * Slot: name Description: The name or title of the compliance check. For example, CIS: <code>Ensure mounting of cramfs filesystems is disabled</code> or DISA STIG: <code>The Ubuntu operating system must implement DoD-approved encryption to protect the confidentiality of remote access sessions</code>.
--     * Slot: severity Description: The severity level as defined in the source document. For example CIS Benchmarks, valid values are: <code>Level 1</code> (security-forward, essential settings), <code>Level 2</code> (security-focused environment, more restrictive), or <code>Scored/Not Scored</code> (whether compliance can be automatically checked). For DISA STIG, valid values are: <code>CAT I</code> (maps to severity_id 5/Critical), <code>CAT II</code> (maps to severity_id 4/High), or <code>CAT III</code> (maps to severity_id 3/Medium).
--     * Slot: severity_id Description: The normalized severity identifier that maps severity levels to standard severity levels. For example CIS Benchmark: <code>Level 2</code> maps to <code>4</code> (High), <code>Level 1</code> maps to <code>3</code> (Medium). For DISA STIG: <code>CAT I</code> maps to <code>5</code> (Critical), <code>CAT II</code> maps to <code>4</code> (High), and <code>CAT III</code> maps to <code>3</code> (Medium).
--     * Slot: status Description: The resultant status of the compliance check normalized to the caption of the <code>status_id</code> value. For example, CIS Benchmark: <code>Pass</code> when all requirements are met, <code>Fail</code> when requirements are not met, or DISA STIG: <code>NotAFinding</code> (maps to status_id 1/Pass), <code>Open</code> (maps to status_id 3/Fail).
--     * Slot: status_id Description: The normalized status identifier of the compliance check.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the compliance check within its standard or framework. For example, CIS Benchmark identifier <code>1.1.1.1</code>, DISA STIG identifier <code>V-230234</code>, or NIST control identifier <code>AC-17(2)</code>.
--     * Slot: version Description: The check version. For example, CIS Benchmark: <code>1.1.0</code> for Amazon Linux 2 or DISA STIG: <code>V2R1</code> for Windows 10.
--     * Slot: OcsfCompliance_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: resource_id Description: Describes details about the resource that this check evaluated.
-- # Class: OcsfCisBenchmark Description: The CIS Benchmark object describes best practices for securely configuring IT systems, software, networks, and cloud infrastructure as defined by the Center for Internet Security. See also Getting to Know the CIS Benchmarks.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: desc Description: The CIS Benchmark description. For example: <i>The cramfs filesystem type is a compressed read-only Linux filesystem embedded in small footprint systems. A cramfs image can be used without having to first decompress the image.</i>
--     * Slot: name Description: The CIS Benchmark name. For example: <i>Ensure mounting of cramfs filesystems is disabled.</i>
-- # Class: OcsfCisBenchmarkResult Description: The CIS Benchmark Result object contains information as defined by the Center for Internet Security (CIS) benchmark result. CIS Benchmarks are a collection of best practices for securely configuring IT systems, software, networks, and cloud infrastructure.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: desc Description: The CIS benchmark description.
--     * Slot: name Description: The CIS benchmark name.
--     * Slot: remediation_id Description: Describes the recommended remediation steps to address identified issue(s).
--     * Slot: rule_id Description: The CIS benchmark rule.
-- # Class: OcsfCisControl Description: The CIS Control (aka Critical Security Control) object describes a prioritized set of actions to protect your organization and data from cyber-attack vectors. The CIS Controls are defined by the Center for Internet Security.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: desc Description: The CIS Control description. For example: <i>Uninstall or disable unnecessary services on enterprise assets and software, such as an unused file sharing service, web application module, or service function.</i>
--     * Slot: name Description: The CIS Control name. For example: <i>4.8 Uninstall or Disable Unnecessary Services on Enterprise Assets and Software.</i>
--     * Slot: version Description: The CIS Control version. For example: <i>v8</i>.
--     * Slot: OcsfCisBenchmark_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfRemediation_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
-- # Class: OcsfCisCsc Description: The CIS Critical Security Control (CSC) contains information as defined by the Center for Internet Security Critical Security Controls (CIS CSC). Prioritized set of actions to protect your organization and data from cyber-attack vectors.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: control Description: A Control is prescriptive, prioritized, and simplified set of best practices that one can use to strengthen their cybersecurity posture. e.g. AWS SecurityHub Controls, CIS Controls.
--     * Slot: version Description: The CIS critical security control version.
--     * Slot: OcsfSecurityFinding_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
-- # Class: OcsfClassifierDetails Description: The Classifier Details object describes details about the classifier used for data classification.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: name Description: The name of the classifier.
--     * Slot: type Description: The type of the classifier.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the classifier.
-- # Class: OcsfClipboard Description: The Clipboard object represents an endpoint clipboard and its contents.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: name Description: The name of the clipboard on systems with named clipboards. For example: <code>General</code> or <code>Drag</code>.
-- # Class: OcsfClipboardItem Description: A piece of data on the system clipboard.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: binary_data Description: The item's data in binary form, used when the clipboard content cannot be represented as text or when preserving the exact binary representation is required.
--     * Slot: clipboard_native_type Description: The type of the item's data, in an operating specific form. For example: <code>CF_TEXT</code> or <code>public.utf8-plain-text</code>.
--     * Slot: mime_type Description: The Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (MIME) type of the item's data. For example: <code>text/plain</code>.
--     * Slot: string_data Description: The item's data if it can be represented as a UTF-8 string.
--     * Slot: OcsfClipboard_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
-- # Class: OcsfCloud Description: The Cloud object describes the cloud computing environment where an event or finding originated. It provides comprehensive context about the cloud infrastructure, including the cloud service provider, account or subscription details, organizational structure, geographic regions, availability zones, and logical partitions.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: cloud_partition Description: The logical grouping or isolated segment within a cloud provider's infrastructure where the event or finding was created, often used for compliance, governance, or regional separation.<br/><br/><strong>Examples:</strong><ul><li><strong>AWS:</strong> Partition where the event occurred (<code>aws</code>, <code>aws-cn</code>, <code>aws-us-gov</code>)</li><li><strong>Azure:</strong> Cloud environment where the event occurred (<code>AzureCloud</code>, <code>AzureUSGovernment</code>, <code>AzureChinaCloud</code>)</li></ul>
--     * Slot: project_uid Description: The unique identifier of a Cloud project.
--     * Slot: provider Description: The unique name of the Cloud services provider where the event or finding was created. Examples include AWS, Azure, GCP (Google Cloud Platform), Oracle Cloud, IBM Cloud, Alibaba Cloud, or other public, private, or hybrid cloud providers.
--     * Slot: region Description: The cloud region where the event or finding was created, as defined by the cloud provider.<br/><br/><strong>Examples:</strong><ul><li><strong>AWS:</strong> Region where the event occurred (<code>us-east-1</code>, <code>eu-west-1</code>)</li><li><strong>Azure:</strong> Region where the event occurred (<code>East US</code>, <code>West Europe</code>)</li><li><strong>GCP:</strong> Region where the event occurred (<code>us-central1</code>, <code>europe-west1</code>)</li><li><strong>Oracle Cloud:</strong> Region where the event occurred (<code>us-ashburn-1</code>, <code>uk-london-1</code>)</li></ul>
--     * Slot: zone Description: The availability zone in the cloud region where the event or finding was created, as defined by the cloud provider.<br/><br/><strong>Examples:</strong><ul><li><strong>AWS:</strong> Availability zone where the event occurred (<code>us-east-1a</code>, <code>us-east-1b</code>)</li><li><strong>Azure:</strong> Availability zone where the event occurred (<code>1</code>, <code>2</code>, <code>3</code> within a region)</li><li><strong>GCP:</strong> Availability zone where the event occurred (<code>us-central1-a</code>, <code>us-central1-b</code>)</li><li><strong>Oracle Cloud:</strong> Availability zone where the event occurred (<code>AD-1</code>, <code>AD-2</code>, <code>AD-3</code>)</li></ul>
--     * Slot: account_id Description: The Account object containing details about the cloud account, subscription, or billing unit where the event or finding was created. This object includes properties such as the account name, unique identifier, type, labels, and tags.<br/><br/><strong>Examples:</strong><ul><li><strong>AWS:</strong> Account object with <code>name</code>, <code>uid</code> (Account ID), <code>type</code>, and other account properties</li><li><strong>Azure:</strong> Subscription object with <code>name</code>, <code>uid</code> (Subscription ID), <code>type</code>, and subscription metadata</li><li><strong>GCP:</strong> Project object with <code>name</code>, <code>uid</code> (Project ID), <code>type</code>, and project attributes</li><li><strong>Oracle Cloud:</strong> Compartment object with <code>name</code>, <code>uid</code> (Tenancy OCID), <code>type</code>, and compartment details</li></ul>
--     * Slot: org_id Description: The Organization object containing details about the organizational unit or management structure that governs the account, subscription, or project where the event or finding was created. This object includes properties such as the organization name, unique identifier, type, and other organizational metadata.<br/><br/><strong>Examples:</strong><ul><li><strong>AWS:</strong> Organization object with <code>name</code>, <code>uid</code> (Organization ID), <code>type</code>, and other organizational properties</li><li><strong>Azure:</strong> Management Group object with <code>name</code>, <code>uid</code> (Management Group ID), <code>type</code>, and management group metadata</li><li><strong>GCP:</strong> Organization object with <code>name</code>, <code>uid</code> (Organization ID), <code>type</code>, and organizational attributes</li><li><strong>Oracle Cloud:</strong> Tenancy object with <code>name</code>, <code>uid</code> (Tenancy OCID), <code>type</code>, and tenancy details</li></ul>
-- # Class: OcsfCompliance Description: The Compliance object contains information about Industry and Regulatory Framework standards, controls and requirements or details about custom assessments utilized in a compliance evaluation. Standards define broad security frameworks, controls represent specific security requirements within those frameworks, and checks are the testable verification points used to determine if controls are properly implemented.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: category Description: The category a control framework pertains to, as reported by the source tool, such as <code>Asset Management</code> or <code>Risk Assessment</code>.
--     * Slot: control Description: A Control is a prescriptive, actionable set of specifications that strengthens device posture. The control specifies required security measures, while the specific implementation values are defined in control_parameters. E.g., CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark 1.2.0 - Control 2.1 - Ensure CloudTrail is enabled in all regions
--     * Slot: desc Description: The description or criteria of a control.
--     * Slot: status Description: The resultant status of the compliance check normalized to the caption of the <code>status_id</code> value. In the case of 'Other', it is defined by the event source.
--     * Slot: status_code Description: The resultant status code of the compliance check.
--     * Slot: status_detail Description: The contextual description of the <code>status, status_code</code> values.
--     * Slot: status_id Description: The normalized status identifier of the compliance check.
-- # Class: OcsfContainer Description: The Container object describes an instance of a specific container. A container is a prepackaged, portable system image that runs isolated on an existing system using a container runtime like containerd.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: name Description: The container name.
--     * Slot: network_driver Description: The network driver used by the container. For example, bridge, overlay, host, none, etc.
--     * Slot: orchestrator Description: The orchestrator managing the container, such as ECS, EKS, K8s, or OpenShift.
--     * Slot: pod_uuid Description: The unique identifier of the pod (or equivalent) that the container is executing on.
--     * Slot: runtime Description: The backend running the container, such as containerd or cri-o.
--     * Slot: size Description: The size of the container image.
--     * Slot: tag Description: The tag used by the container. It can indicate version, format, OS.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The full container unique identifier for this instantiation of the container. For example: <code>ac2ea168264a08f9aaca0dfc82ff3551418dfd22d02b713142a6843caa2f61bf</code>.
--     * Slot: OcsfRequest_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfResponse_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: hash_id Description: Commit hash of image created for docker or the SHA256 hash of the container. For example: <code>13550340a8681c84c861aac2e5b440161c2b33a3e4f302ac680ca5b686de48de</code>.
--     * Slot: image_id Description: The container image used as a template to run the container.
-- # Class: OcsfCpuInfo Description: The CPU information object contains attributes describing a physical CPU package installed in the device.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: cores Description: The number of processing cores or compute units for the component.
--     * Slot: cpu_architecture Description: The CPU architecture, normalized to the caption of the <code>cpu_architecture_id</code> value. In the case of <code>Other</code>, it is defined by the source.
--     * Slot: cpu_architecture_id Description: The normalized identifier of the CPU architecture.
--     * Slot: cpu_bits Description: The number of bits used by the CPU for memory addressing.
--     * Slot: model Description: The model name of the CPU. For example: <code>Intel Xeon Gold 6348</code>, <code>AMD EPYC 7763</code>, or <code>Apple M3 Max</code>.
--     * Slot: speed_mhz Description: The nominal clock speed of the unit, expressed in megahertz.
--     * Slot: vendor_name Description: The name of the vendor of the CPU. For example: <code>Intel</code>, <code>AMD</code>, or <code>Apple</code>.
--     * Slot: OcsfDeviceHwInfo_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
-- # Class: OcsfCve Description: The Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures (CVE) object represents publicly disclosed cybersecurity vulnerabilities defined in CVE Program catalog (CVE). There is one CVE Record for each vulnerability in the catalog.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: created_time Description: The Record Creation Date identifies when the CVE ID was issued to a CVE Numbering Authority (CNA) or the CVE Record was published on the CVE List. Note that the Record Creation Date does not necessarily indicate when this vulnerability was discovered, shared with the affected vendor, publicly disclosed, or updated in CVE.
--     * Slot: created_time_dt Description: The Record Creation Date identifies when the CVE ID was issued to a CVE Numbering Authority (CNA) or the CVE Record was published on the CVE List. Note that the Record Creation Date does not necessarily indicate when this vulnerability was discovered, shared with the affected vendor, publicly disclosed, or updated in CVE.
--     * Slot: cwe_uid Description: The Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE) unique identifier. For example: <code>CWE-787</code>.
--     * Slot: cwe_url Description: Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE) definition URL. For example: <code>https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html</code>.
--     * Slot: desc Description: A brief description of the CVE Record.
--     * Slot: modified_time Description: The Record Modified Date identifies when the CVE record was last updated.
--     * Slot: modified_time_dt Description: The Record Modified Date identifies when the CVE record was last updated.
--     * Slot: title Description: A title or a brief phrase summarizing the CVE record.
--     * Slot: type Description: <p>The vulnerability type as classified in the CVE catalog.</p>Most frequently used vulnerability types are: <code>DoS</code>, <code>Code Execution</code>, <code>Overflow</code>, <code>Memory Corruption</code>, <code>Sql Injection</code>, <code>XSS</code>, <code>Directory Traversal</code>, <code>Http Response Splitting</code>, <code>Bypass something</code>, <code>Gain Information</code>, <code>Gain Privileges</code>, <code>CSRF</code>, <code>File Inclusion</code>. For more information see Vulnerabilities By Type distributions.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures unique number assigned to a specific computer vulnerability. A CVE Identifier begins with 4 digits representing the year followed by a sequence of digits that acts as a unique identifier. For example: <code>CVE-2021-12345</code>.
--     * Slot: OcsfAdvisory_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfMalware_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: cwe_id Description: The CWE object represents a weakness in a software system that can be exploited by a threat actor to perform an attack. The CWE object is based on the Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE) catalog.
--     * Slot: epss_id Description: The Exploit Prediction Scoring System (EPSS) object describes the estimated probability a vulnerability will be exploited. EPSS is a community-driven effort to combine descriptive information about vulnerabilities (CVEs) with evidence of actual exploitation in-the-wild.
--     * Slot: product_id Description: The product where the vulnerability was discovered.
-- # Class: OcsfCvss Description: The Common Vulnerability Scoring System (CVSS) object provides a way to capture the principal characteristics of a vulnerability and produce a numerical score reflecting its severity.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: base_score Description: The CVSS base score. For example: <code>9.1</code>.
--     * Slot: depth Description: The CVSS depth represents a depth of the equation used to calculate CVSS score.
--     * Slot: overall_score Description: The CVSS overall score, impacted by base, temporal, and environmental metrics. For example: <code>9.1</code>.
--     * Slot: severity Description: <p>The Common Vulnerability Scoring System (CVSS) Qualitative Severity Rating. A textual representation of the numeric score.</p><strong>CVSS v2.0</strong><ul><li>Low (0.0 – 3.9)</li><li>Medium (4.0 – 6.9)</li><li>High (7.0 – 10.0)</li></ul></p><strong>CVSS v3.0</strong><ul><li>None (0.0)</li><li>Low (0.1 - 3.9)</li><li>Medium (4.0 - 6.9)</li><li>High (7.0 - 8.9)</li><li>Critical (9.0 - 10.0)</li></ul>
--     * Slot: src_url Description: The source URL for the CVSS score. For example: <code>https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2021-44228</code>
--     * Slot: vector_string Description: The CVSS vector string is a text representation of a set of CVSS metrics. It is commonly used to record or transfer CVSS metric information in a concise form. For example: <code>3.1/AV:L/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:N/A:H</code>.
--     * Slot: vendor_name Description: The vendor that provided the CVSS score. For example: <code>NVD, REDHAT</code> etc.
--     * Slot: version Description: The CVSS version. For example: <code>3.1</code>.
--     * Slot: OcsfCve_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
-- # Class: OcsfCwe Description: The CWE object represents a weakness in a software system that can be exploited by a threat actor to perform an attack. The CWE object is based on the Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE) catalog.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: caption Description: The caption assigned to the Common Weakness Enumeration unique identifier.
--     * Slot: src_url Description: URL pointing to the CWE Specification. For more information see <a target='_blank' href='https://cwe.mitre.org/'>CWE.</a>
--     * Slot: uid Description: The Common Weakness Enumeration unique number assigned to a specific weakness. A CWE Identifier begins "CWE" followed by a sequence of digits that acts as a unique identifier. For example: <code>CWE-123</code>.
--     * Slot: OcsfAdvisory_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfCve_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
-- # Class: OcsfD3fTactic Description: The Defensive Tactic object describes the tactic ID and/or name that is associated to an attack.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: src_url Description: The versioned permalink of the defensive tactic. For example: <code>https://d3fend.mitre.org/tactic/d3f:Isolate/</code>
--     * Slot: name Description: The tactic name that is associated with the defensive technique. For example: <code>Isolate</code>.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the defensive tactic.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
-- # Class: OcsfD3fTechnique Description: The Defensive Technique object describes the leaf defensive technique ID and/or name associated to a countermeasure.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: src_url Description: The versioned permalink of the defensive technique. For example: <code>https://d3fend.mitre.org/technique/d3f:IOPortRestriction/</code>
--     * Slot: name Description: The name of the defensive technique. For example: <code>IO Port Restriction</code>.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the defensive technique. For example: <code>D3-IOPR</code>.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
-- # Class: OcsfD3fend Description: The Defensive Countermeasure object describes the tactic & technique associated with a countermeasure.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: version Description: The D3FEND™ Matrix version.
--     * Slot: OcsfMitigation_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfRemediationActivity_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfFileRemediationActivity_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfNetworkRemediationActivity_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfProcessRemediationActivity_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: d3f_tactic_id Description: The Tactic object describes the tactic ID and/or name that is associated with a countermeasure.
--     * Slot: d3f_technique_id Description: The Technique object describes the technique ID and/or name associated with a countermeasure.
-- # Class: OcsfDataClassification Description: The Data Classification object includes information about data classification levels and data category types.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: category Description: The name of the data classification category that data matched into, e.g. Financial, Personal, Governmental, etc.
--     * Slot: category_id Description: The normalized identifier of the data classification category.
--     * Slot: confidentiality Description: The file content confidentiality, normalized to the confidentiality_id value. In the case of 'Other', it is defined by the event source.
--     * Slot: confidentiality_id Description: The normalized identifier of the file content confidentiality indicator.
--     * Slot: size Description: Size of the data classified.
--     * Slot: src_url Description: The source URL pointing towards the full classification job details.
--     * Slot: status Description: The resultant status of the classification job normalized to the caption of the <code>status_id</code> value. In the case of 'Other', it is defined by the event source.
--     * Slot: status_id Description: The normalized status identifier of the classification job.
--     * Slot: total Description: The total count of discovered entities, by the classification job.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the classification job.
--     * Slot: OcsfDataClassificationProfile_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfResource_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfDatabase_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfDatabucket_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfEmail_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfFile_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfMetadata_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfProduct_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfResourceDetails_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfWebResource_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfWinResource_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: classifier_details_id Description: Describes details about the classifier used for data classification.
--     * Slot: policy_id Description: Details about the data policy that governs data handling and security measures related to classification.
-- # Class: OcsfDataSecurity Description: The Data Security object describes the characteristics, techniques and content of a Data Loss Prevention (DLP), Data Loss Detection (DLD), Data Classification, or similar tools' finding, alert, or detection mechanism(s).
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: data_lifecycle_state Description: The name of the stage or state that the data was in. E.g., Data-at-Rest, Data-in-Transit, etc.
--     * Slot: data_lifecycle_state_id Description: The stage or state that the data was in when it was assessed or scanned by a data security tool.
--     * Slot: detection_pattern Description: Specific pattern, algorithm, fingerprint, or model used for detection.
--     * Slot: detection_system Description: The data security tool or system that the finding, detection, or alert originated from, normalized to the caption of <code>detection_system_id</code>. E.g., Endpoint, Secure Email Gateway, etc.
--     * Slot: detection_system_id Description: The normalized identifier of the data security tool or system type from which the finding, detection, or alert originated. When the type is not listed, use <code>99</code> (Other) and populate <code>detection_system</code> with the source-specific label.
--     * Slot: pattern_match Description: A text, binary, file name, or datastore that matched against a detection rule.
--     * Slot: category Description: The name of the data classification category that data matched into, e.g. Financial, Personal, Governmental, etc.
--     * Slot: category_id Description: The normalized identifier of the data classification category.
--     * Slot: confidentiality Description: The file content confidentiality, normalized to the confidentiality_id value. In the case of 'Other', it is defined by the event source.
--     * Slot: confidentiality_id Description: The normalized identifier of the file content confidentiality indicator.
--     * Slot: size Description: Size of the data classified.
--     * Slot: src_url Description: The source URL pointing towards the full classification job details.
--     * Slot: status Description: The resultant status of the classification job normalized to the caption of the <code>status_id</code> value. In the case of 'Other', it is defined by the event source.
--     * Slot: status_id Description: The normalized status identifier of the classification job.
--     * Slot: total Description: The total count of discovered entities, by the classification job.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the classification job.
--     * Slot: classifier_details_id Description: Describes details about the classifier used for data classification.
--     * Slot: policy_id Description: Details about the policy that triggered the finding.
-- # Class: OcsfDatabase Description: The database object is used for databases which are typically datastore services that contain an organized collection of structured and unstructured data or a types of data.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: created_time Description: The time when the database was known to have been created.
--     * Slot: created_time_dt Description: The time when the database was known to have been created.
--     * Slot: desc Description: The description of the database.
--     * Slot: embedding_model Description: Model used for creating embeddings (if applicable). For example: <code>text-embedding-ada-002</code> or <code>all-MiniLM-L6-v2</code>.
--     * Slot: modified_time Description: The most recent time when any changes, updates, or modifications were made within the database.
--     * Slot: modified_time_dt Description: The most recent time when any changes, updates, or modifications were made within the database.
--     * Slot: size Description: The size of the database in bytes.
--     * Slot: type Description: The database type.
--     * Slot: type_id Description: The normalized identifier of the database type.
--     * Slot: name Description: The database name, ordinarily as assigned by a database administrator.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the database.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
--     * Slot: data_classification_id Description: The Data Classification object includes information about data classification levels and data category types.
-- # Class: OcsfDatabucket Description: The databucket object is a basic container that holds data, typically organized through the use of data partitions.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: cloud_partition Description: The logical grouping or isolated segment within a cloud provider's infrastructure where the databucket is located.
--     * Slot: created_time_dt Description: The time when the databucket was known to have been created.
--     * Slot: criticality Description: The criticality of the databucket as defined by the event source.
--     * Slot: desc Description: The description of the databucket.
--     * Slot: hostname Description: The fully qualified hostname of the databucket.
--     * Slot: ip Description: The IP address of the resource, in either IPv4 or IPv6 format.
--     * Slot: is_backed_up Description: Indicates whether the device or resource has a backup enabled, such as an automated snapshot or a cloud backup. For example, this is indicated by the <code>cloudBackupEnabled</code> value within JAMF Pro mobile devices or the registration of an AWS ARN with the AWS Backup service.
--     * Slot: is_encrypted Description: Indicates if the databucket is encrypted.
--     * Slot: is_public Description: Indicates if the databucket is publicly accessible.
--     * Slot: modified_time_dt Description: The most recent time when any changes, updates, or modifications were made within the databucket.
--     * Slot: namespace Description: The namespace is useful when similar entities exist that you need to keep separate.
--     * Slot: region Description: The cloud region of the databucket.
--     * Slot: size Description: The size of the databucket in bytes.
--     * Slot: type_id Description: The normalized identifier of the databucket type.
--     * Slot: version Description: The version of the resource. For example <code>1.2.3</code>.
--     * Slot: zone Description: The specific availability zone within a cloud region where the databucket is located.
--     * Slot: created_time Description: The time when the databucket was known to have been created.
--     * Slot: data Description: Additional data describing the resource.
--     * Slot: modified_time Description: The most recent time when any changes, updates, or modifications were made within the databucket.
--     * Slot: type Description: The databucket type.
--     * Slot: uid_alt Description: The alternative unique identifier of the resource.
--     * Slot: name Description: The databucket name.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the databucket.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
--     * Slot: encryption_details_id Description: The encryption details of the databucket. Should be populated if the databucket is encrypted.
--     * Slot: file_id Description: Details about the file/object within a databucket.
--     * Slot: group_id Description: The name of the related resource group.
--     * Slot: owner_id Description: The identity of the service or user account that owns the databucket.
--     * Slot: resource_relationship_id Description: A graph representation showing how this databucket relates to and interacts with other entities in the environment. This can include parent/child relationships, dependencies, or other connections.
--     * Slot: data_classification_id Description: The Data Classification object includes information about data classification levels and data category types.
-- # Class: OcsfDceRpc Description: The DCE/RPC, or Distributed Computing Environment/Remote Procedure Call, object describes the remote procedure call system for distributed computing environments.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: command Description: The request command (e.g. REQUEST, BIND).
--     * Slot: command_response Description: The reply to the request command (e.g. RESPONSE, BINDACK or FAULT).
--     * Slot: opnum Description: An operation number used to identify a specific remote procedure call (RPC) method or a method in an interface.
--     * Slot: rpc_interface_id Description: The RPC Interface object describes the details pertaining to the remote procedure call interface.
-- # Class: OcsfDelegation Description: A durable authorization context that a principal issues to a delegate (e.g., an application, automated process, service account, or AI agent), representing delegated authority to act on the principal's behalf. Persists independently of any single trace, session, or workflow instance, so the same delegation can be referenced across many events. The principal and delegate themselves are identified by the other objects on the event carrying this delegation, such as <code>actor.user</code> or <code>actor.iam_role</code> for the principal and <code>ai_operation.ai_agent</code> for an AI delegate. The <code>delegation</code> object itself only carries the durable identifiers used to correlate across those events. Delegations reference a parent via <code>parent_uid</code>, forming a directed acyclic graph (DAG) of re-delegations that supports lineage queries across the chain of authority. This authority graph is distinct from any agent instantiation or orchestration hierarchy, which may or may not coincide with it.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: created_time Description: The time when the delegation was issued by the issuing authority.
--     * Slot: created_time_dt Description: The time when the delegation was issued by the issuing authority.
--     * Slot: issuer_uid Description: The unique identifier of the trusted issuing authority (e.g., an OAuth authorization server, cloud STS, IAM broker, orchestration gateway, or SDK wrapper) that generated this delegation. Enables verification that delegation IDs were issued by an authorized component rather than self-asserted by the delegate.
--     * Slot: parent_uid Description: The delegation identifier of the parent delegation, if this delegation was created by re-delegating authority to another party (e.g., a downstream service or automated process). This creates a parent-child relationship that enables tracking the complete chain of delegation ancestry. Does not indicate that the delegate was instantiated or spawned by the parent's delegate; that relationship, if any, is tracked separately.
--     * Slot: uid Description: Unique identifier for this delegation context. Must be stable across the lifetime of the delegation and generated by a trusted issuing authority (e.g., an OAuth authorization server, cloud STS, IAM broker, or orchestration gateway) rather than self-asserted by the delegate. All events executed under this delegation share this identifier.
-- # Class: OcsfEndpoint Description: The Endpoint object describes a physical or virtual device that connects to and exchanges information with a computer network. Some examples of endpoints are mobile devices, desktop computers, virtual machines, embedded devices, and servers. Internet-of-Things devices—like cameras, lighting, refrigerators, security systems, smart speakers, and thermostats—are also endpoints.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: domain Description: The name of the domain that the endpoint belongs to or that corresponds to the endpoint.
--     * Slot: hostname Description: The fully qualified name of the endpoint.
--     * Slot: instance_uid Description: The unique identifier of a VM instance.
--     * Slot: interface_name Description: The name of the network interface (e.g. eth2).
--     * Slot: interface_uid Description: The unique identifier of the network interface.
--     * Slot: ip Description: The IP address of the endpoint, in either IPv4 or IPv6 format.
--     * Slot: mac Description: The Media Access Control (MAC) address of the endpoint.
--     * Slot: mac_vendor Description: The vendor or manufacturer of the endpoint's network interface controller (NIC), as identified from the MAC address.
--     * Slot: subnet_uid Description: The unique identifier of a virtual subnet.
--     * Slot: type Description: The endpoint type. For example: <code>unknown</code>, <code>server</code>, <code>desktop</code>, <code>laptop</code>, <code>tablet</code>, <code>mobile</code>, <code>virtual</code>, <code>browser</code>, or <code>other</code>.
--     * Slot: type_id Description: The endpoint type ID.
--     * Slot: vlan_uid Description: The Virtual LAN identifier.
--     * Slot: vpc_uid Description: The unique identifier of the Virtual Private Cloud (VPC).
--     * Slot: zone Description: The network zone or LAN segment.
--     * Slot: namespace_pid Description: If running under a process namespace (such as in a container), the process identifier within that process namespace.
--     * Slot: name Description: The short name of the endpoint.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the endpoint.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
--     * Slot: hw_info_id Description: The endpoint hardware information.
--     * Slot: location_id Description: The geographical location of the endpoint.
--     * Slot: os_id Description: The endpoint operating system.
--     * Slot: owner_id Description: The identity of the service or user account that owns the endpoint or was last logged into it.
--     * Slot: pool_id Description: The pool of desktops or virtual machines to which the endpoint belongs.
--     * Slot: container_id Description: The information describing an instance of a container. A container is a prepackaged, portable system image that runs isolated on an existing system using a container runtime like containerd.
-- # Class: OcsfDevice Description: The Device object represents an addressable computer system or host, which is typically connected to a computer network and participates in the transmission or processing of data within the computer network.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: autoscale_uid Description: The unique identifier of the cloud autoscale configuration.
--     * Slot: boot_time Description: The time the system was booted.
--     * Slot: boot_time_dt Description: The time the system was booted.
--     * Slot: boot_uid Description: A unique identifier of the device that changes after every reboot. For example, the value of <code>/proc/sys/kernel/random/boot_id</code> from Linux's procfs.
--     * Slot: created_time Description: The time when the device was known to have been created.
--     * Slot: created_time_dt Description: The time when the device was known to have been created.
--     * Slot: desc Description: The description of the device, ordinarily as reported by the operating system.
--     * Slot: eid Description: An Embedded Identity Document, is a unique serial number that identifies an eSIM-enabled device.
--     * Slot: first_seen_time Description: The initial discovery time of the device.
--     * Slot: first_seen_time_dt Description: The initial discovery time of the device.
--     * Slot: hypervisor Description: The name of the hypervisor running on the device. For example, <code>Xen</code>, <code>VMware</code>, <code>Hyper-V</code>, <code>VirtualBox</code>, etc.
--     * Slot: iccid Description: The Integrated Circuit Card Identification of a mobile device. Typically it is a unique 18 to 22 digit number that identifies a SIM card.
--     * Slot: imei Description: The International Mobile Equipment Identity that is associated with the device.
--     * Slot: is_backed_up Description: Indicates whether the device or resource has a backup enabled, such as an automated snapshot or a cloud backup. For example, this is indicated by the <code>cloudBackupEnabled</code> value within JAMF Pro mobile devices or the registration of an AWS ARN with the AWS Backup service.
--     * Slot: is_compliant Description: The event occurred on a compliant device.
--     * Slot: is_managed Description: The event occurred on a managed device.
--     * Slot: is_mobile_account_active Description: Indicates whether the device has an active mobile account. For example, this is indicated by the <code>itunesStoreAccountActive</code> value within JAMF Pro mobile devices.
--     * Slot: is_personal Description: The event occurred on a personal device.
--     * Slot: is_shared Description: The event occurred on a shared device.
--     * Slot: is_supervised Description: The event occurred on a supervised device. Devices that are supervised are typically mobile devices managed by a Mobile Device Management solution and are restricted from specific behaviors such as Apple AirDrop.
--     * Slot: is_trusted Description: The event occurred on a trusted device.
--     * Slot: last_seen_time Description: The most recent discovery time of the device.
--     * Slot: last_seen_time_dt Description: The most recent discovery time of the device.
--     * Slot: meid Description: The Mobile Equipment Identifier. It's a unique number that identifies a Code Division Multiple Access (CDMA) mobile device.
--     * Slot: model Description: The model of the device. For example <code>ThinkPad X1 Carbon</code>.
--     * Slot: modified_time Description: The time when the device was last known to have been modified.
--     * Slot: modified_time_dt Description: The time when the device was last known to have been modified.
--     * Slot: os_machine_uuid Description: The operating system assigned Machine ID. In Windows, this is the value stored at the registry path: <code>HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Cryptography\MachineGuid</code>. In Linux, this is stored in the file: <code>/etc/machine-id</code>.
--     * Slot: region Description: The region where the virtual machine is located. For example, an AWS Region.
--     * Slot: risk_level Description: The risk level, normalized to the caption of the risk_level_id value.
--     * Slot: risk_level_id Description: The normalized risk level id.
--     * Slot: risk_score Description: The risk score as reported by the event source.
--     * Slot: subnet Description: The subnet mask.
--     * Slot: udid Description: The Apple assigned Unique Device Identifier (UDID). For iOS, iPadOS, tvOS, watchOS and visionOS devices, this is the UDID. For macOS devices, it is the Provisioning UDID. For example: <code>00008020-008D4548007B4F26</code>
--     * Slot: uid_alt Description: An alternate unique identifier of the device if any. For example the ActiveDirectory DN.
--     * Slot: vendor_name Description: The vendor for the device. For example <code>Dell</code> or <code>Lenovo</code>.
--     * Slot: domain Description: The network domain where the device resides. For example: <code>work.example.com</code>.
--     * Slot: hostname Description: The device hostname.
--     * Slot: instance_uid Description: The unique identifier of a VM instance.
--     * Slot: interface_name Description: The name of the network interface (e.g. eth2).
--     * Slot: interface_uid Description: The unique identifier of the network interface.
--     * Slot: ip Description: The device IP address, in either IPv4 or IPv6 format.
--     * Slot: mac Description: The Media Access Control (MAC) address of the endpoint.
--     * Slot: mac_vendor Description: The vendor or manufacturer of the endpoint's network interface controller (NIC), as identified from the MAC address.
--     * Slot: subnet_uid Description: The unique identifier of a virtual subnet.
--     * Slot: type Description: The device type. For example: <code>unknown</code>, <code>server</code>, <code>desktop</code>, <code>laptop</code>, <code>tablet</code>, <code>mobile</code>, <code>virtual</code>, <code>browser</code>, or <code>other</code>.
--     * Slot: type_id Description: The device type ID.
--     * Slot: vlan_uid Description: The Virtual LAN identifier.
--     * Slot: vpc_uid Description: The unique identifier of the Virtual Private Cloud (VPC).
--     * Slot: zone Description: The network zone or LAN segment.
--     * Slot: namespace_pid Description: If running under a process namespace (such as in a container), the process identifier within that process namespace.
--     * Slot: name Description: The alternate device name, ordinarily as assigned by an administrator. <p><b>Note:</b> The <b>Name</b> could be any other string that helps to identify the device, such as a phone number; for example <code>310-555-1234</code>.</p>
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the device. For example the Windows TargetSID or AWS EC2 ARN.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
--     * Slot: image_id Description: The image used as a template to run the virtual machine.
--     * Slot: org_id Description: Organization and org unit related to the device.
--     * Slot: hw_info_id Description: The endpoint hardware information.
--     * Slot: location_id Description: The geographical location of the device.
--     * Slot: os_id Description: The endpoint operating system.
--     * Slot: owner_id Description: The identity of the service or user account that owns the endpoint or was last logged into it.
--     * Slot: pool_id Description: The pool of desktops or virtual machines to which the endpoint belongs.
--     * Slot: container_id Description: The information describing an instance of a container. A container is a prepackaged, portable system image that runs isolated on an existing system using a container runtime like containerd.
-- # Class: OcsfDeviceHwInfo Description: The Device Hardware Information object contains details and specifications of the physical components that make up a device. This information provides an overview of the hardware capabilities, configuration, and characteristics of the device.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: bios_date Description: The BIOS date. For example: <code>03/31/16</code>.
--     * Slot: bios_manufacturer Description: The BIOS manufacturer. For example: <code>LENOVO</code>.
--     * Slot: bios_ver Description: The BIOS version. For example: <code>LENOVO G5ETA2WW (2.62)</code>.
--     * Slot: chassis Description: The chassis type describes the system enclosure or physical form factor. For example, as described for a Windows chassis type.
--     * Slot: cores Description: The total number of processor cores across all installed CPUs on the system. For per-CPU core detail, use <code>cpu_info_list</code>.
--     * Slot: cpu_architecture Description: The CPU architecture, normalized to the caption of the <code>cpu_architecture_id</code> value. In the case of <code>Other</code>, it is defined by the source.
--     * Slot: cpu_architecture_id Description: The normalized identifier of the CPU architecture.
--     * Slot: cpu_bits Description: The cpu architecture, the number of bits used for addressing in memory. For example: <code>32</code> or <code>64</code>.
--     * Slot: cpu_cores Description: The number of processor cores in all installed processors. For Example: <code>42</code>.
--     * Slot: cpu_count Description: The number of physical processors on a system. For per-CPU detail, use <code>cpu_info_list</code>.
--     * Slot: cpu_speed Description: The speed of the processor in Mhz. For Example: <code>4200</code>.
--     * Slot: cpu_type Description: The processor type. For example: <code>x86 Family 6 Model 37 Stepping 5</code>.
--     * Slot: gpu_count Description: The number of graphics processors on a system. For per-GPU detail, use <code>gpu_info_list</code>.
--     * Slot: ram_size Description: The total amount of installed RAM, in Megabytes. For example: <code>2048</code>.
--     * Slot: serial_number Description: The device manufacturer serial number.
--     * Slot: uuid Description: The device manufacturer assigned universally unique hardware identifier. For SMBIOS compatible devices such as those running Linux and Windows, it is the UUID member of the System Information structure in the SMBIOS information. For macOS devices, it is the Hardware UUID (also known as IOPlatformUUID in the I/O Registry).
--     * Slot: vendor_name Description: The device manufacturer.
--     * Slot: desktop_display_id Description: The desktop display affiliated with the event
--     * Slot: keyboard_info_id Description: The keyboard detailed information.
-- # Class: OcsfDigitalSignature Description: The Digital Signature object contains information about the cryptographic mechanism used to verify the authenticity, integrity, and origin of the file or application.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: algorithm Description: The digital signature algorithm used to create the signature, normalized to the caption of 'algorithm_id'. In the case of 'Other', it is defined by the event source.
--     * Slot: algorithm_id Description: The identifier of the normalized digital signature algorithm.
--     * Slot: created_time Description: The time when the digital signature was created.
--     * Slot: created_time_dt Description: The time when the digital signature was created.
--     * Slot: developer_uid Description: The developer ID on the certificate that signed the file.
--     * Slot: serialization Description: The canonical serialization or signing-envelope scheme used to produce the deterministic byte sequence that was signed, normalized to the caption of <code>serialization_id</code>. In the case of <code>Other</code>, it is defined by the event source.
--     * Slot: serialization_id Description: The identifier of the normalized canonical serialization or signing-envelope scheme used to produce the deterministic byte sequence that was signed. A verifier must apply the same scheme to reproduce the signing input. Use <code>Flat</code> where the signed data is an opaque byte sequence, such as file content, to which no canonical serialization was applied. Distinct from <code>algorithm_id</code>, which identifies how the resulting bytes were signed; some signing formats (e.g. <code>Authenticode</code>) define their own canonicalization and populate both fields.
--     * Slot: state Description: The digital signature state defines the signature state, normalized to the caption of 'state_id'. In the case of 'Other', it is defined by the event source.
--     * Slot: state_id Description: The normalized identifier of the signature state.
--     * Slot: OcsfAttestation_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfFile_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfOsint_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: certificate_id Description: The certificate object containing information about the digital certificate.
--     * Slot: digest_id Description: The message digest attribute contains the fixed length message hash representation and the corresponding hashing algorithm information.
-- # Class: OcsfDiscoveryDetails Description: The Discovery Details object describes results of a discovery task/job. Each result may be due to the violation of a <code>rule</code> associated with a <code>policy</code> and have multiple <code>occurrences</code>.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: count Description: The number of discovered entities of the specified type.
--     * Slot: type Description: The specific type of information that was discovered. e.g.<code> name, phone_number, etc.</code>
--     * Slot: value Description: Optionally, the specific value of discovered information.
--     * Slot: OcsfDataClassification_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfDataSecurity_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: occurrence_details_id Description: Details about where in the target entity, specified information was discovered. Only the attributes, relevant to the target entity type should be populated.
--     * Slot: rule_id Description: The rule associated with this discovery, usually part of the higher level <code>policy</code> from an enclosing class or object.
-- # Class: OcsfDisplay Description: The Display object contains information about the physical or virtual display connected to a computer system.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: color_depth Description: The numeric color depth.
--     * Slot: physical_height Description: The numeric physical height of display.
--     * Slot: physical_orientation Description: The numeric physical orientation of display.
--     * Slot: physical_width Description: The numeric physical width of display.
--     * Slot: scale_factor Description: The numeric scale factor of display.
-- # Class: OcsfDnsResourceRecord Description: The DNS Resource Record object represents a single resource record as defined in RFC 1035. It is used in the Answer, Authority, and Additional sections of a DNS message.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: class Description: The class of DNS data contained in this resource record. See RFC 1035. For example: <code>IN</code>.
--     * Slot: hostname Description: The owner name of the resource record. For example: <code>www.example.com</code>.
--     * Slot: rdata Description: The data describing the DNS resource. The meaning of this data depends on the type and class of the resource record.
--     * Slot: ttl Description: The time interval that the resource record may be cached. Zero value means that the resource record can only be used for the transaction in progress, and should not be cached.
--     * Slot: type Description: The type of data contained in this resource record. See RFC 1035. For example: <code>CNAME</code>.
--     * Slot: OcsfDnsSection_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfDnsActivity_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
-- # Class: OcsfDnsAnswer Description: The DNS Answer object represents a specific response provided by the Domain Name System (DNS) when querying for information about a domain or performing a DNS operation. It encapsulates the relevant details and data returned by the DNS server in response to a query.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: packet_uid Description: The DNS packet identifier assigned by the program that generated the query. The identifier is copied to the response.
--     * Slot: class Description: The class of DNS data contained in this resource record. See RFC 1035. For example: <code>IN</code>.
--     * Slot: hostname Description: The owner name of the resource record. For example: <code>www.example.com</code>.
--     * Slot: rdata Description: The data describing the DNS resource. The meaning of this data depends on the type and class of the resource record.
--     * Slot: ttl Description: The time interval that the resource record may be cached. Zero value means that the resource record can only be used for the transaction in progress, and should not be cached.
--     * Slot: type Description: The type of data contained in this resource record. See RFC 1035. For example: <code>CNAME</code>.
--     * Slot: OcsfOsint_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfDnsActivity_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
-- # Class: OcsfDnsQuery Description: The DNS Query object represents a specific request made to the Domain Name System (DNS) to retrieve information about a domain or perform a DNS operation.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: class Description: The class of resource records being queried. See RFC 1035. For example: <code>IN</code>.
--     * Slot: hostname Description: The hostname or domain being queried. For example: <code>www.example.com</code>
--     * Slot: opcode Description: The DNS opcode specifies the type of the query message.
--     * Slot: opcode_id Description: The DNS opcode ID specifies the normalized query message type as defined in RFC 5395.
--     * Slot: packet_uid Description: The DNS packet identifier assigned by the program that generated the query. The identifier is copied to the response.
--     * Slot: type Description: The type of resource records being queried. See RFC 1035. For example: A, AAAA, CNAME, MX, and NS.
-- # Class: OcsfDnsSection Description: The DNS Section object represents a DNS message section that carries supplementary resource records alongside an optional TSIG record. It is used for both the client Additional section (<code>query_additional</code>) and the server Additional section (<code>response_additional</code>) of a DNS message. A DNS message has at most one TSIG record, which authenticates the entire message.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: tsig_id Description: The TSIG (Transaction Signature) record present in this DNS section, used to authenticate the entire DNS message. Per RFC 2845, at most one TSIG record is permitted per DNS message.
-- # Class: OcsfDomainContact Description: The contact information related to a domain registration, e.g., registrant, administrator, abuse, billing, or technical contact.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: email_addr Description: The user's primary email address.
--     * Slot: name Description: The individual or organization name for the contact.
--     * Slot: phone_number Description: The number associated with the phone.
--     * Slot: type Description: The Domain Contact type, normalized to the caption of the <code>type_id</code> value. In the case of 'Other', it is defined by the source
--     * Slot: type_id Description: The normalized domain contact type ID.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the contact information, typically provided in WHOIS information.
--     * Slot: OcsfWhois_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: location_id Description: Location details for the contract such as the city, state/province, country, etc.
-- # Class: OcsfDownloadInfo Description: The Download Info object contains metadata pertaining to a downloaded file.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: referrer Description: The URL that referred to <code>src_url</code>. This is typically the URL of a web page containing a download link.
--     * Slot: src_url Description: The URL from which the file was downloaded.
--     * Slot: type Description: The download type, normalized to the caption of the <code>type_id</code> value. In the case of 'Other', it is defined by the event source.
--     * Slot: type_id Description: The download type ID. The values correspond to the five permitted values of the <code>ZoneId</code> property in the Mark of the Web metadata of a downloaded file on Windows. Note however that each numeric value is 1 greater than its <code>ZoneId</code> equivalent.
--     * Slot: src_endpoint_id Description: Information about the network endpoint from which the file was downloaded.
-- # Class: OcsfEdge Description: Represents a connection or relationship between two nodes in a graph.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: data Description: Additional data about the edge such as weight, distance, or custom properties.
--     * Slot: is_directed Description: Indicates whether the edge is (<code>true</code>) or undirected (<code>false</code>).
--     * Slot: relation Description: The type of relationship between nodes (e.g. is-attached-to , depends-on, etc).
--     * Slot: source Description: The unique identifier of the node where the edge originates.
--     * Slot: target Description: The unique identifier of the node where the edge terminates.
--     * Slot: name Description: The human-readable name or label for the edge.
--     * Slot: uid Description: Unique identifier of the edge.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
--     * Slot: OcsfGraph_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
-- # Class: OcsfEmail Description: The Email object describes the email metadata such as sender, recipients, and direction, and can include embedded URLs and files.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: delivered_to Description: The machine-readable <strong>Delivered-To</strong> email header field. For example <code>example.user@usersdomain.com</code>
--     * Slot: from Description: The machine-readable email header From value, as defined by RFC 5322. For example <code>example.user@usersdomain.com</code>.
--     * Slot: from_mailbox Description: The human-readable email header From Mailbox value. For example <code>'Example User &lt;example.user@usersdomain.com&gt;'</code>.
--     * Slot: is_read Description: The indication of whether the email has been read.
--     * Slot: message_uid Description: The email header Message-ID value, as defined by RFC 5322.
--     * Slot: raw_header Description: The email authentication header.
--     * Slot: reply_to Description: The machine-readable email header Reply-To value, as defined by RFC 5322. For example <code>example.user@usersdomain.com</code>
--     * Slot: return_path Description: The address found in the 'Return-Path' header, which indicates where bounce messages (non-delivery reports) should be sent. This address is often set by the sending system and may differ from the 'From' or 'Sender' addresses. For example, <code>mailer-daemon@senderserver.com</code>.
--     * Slot: sender Description: The machine readable email address of the system or server that actually transmitted the email message, extracted from the email headers per RFC 5322. This differs from the <code>from</code> field, which shows the message author. The sender field is most commonly used when multiple addresses appear in the <code> from_list </code> field, or when the transmitting system is different from the message author (such as when sending on behalf of someone else).
--     * Slot: sender_mailbox Description: The human readable email address of the system or server that actually transmitted the email message, extracted from the email headers per RFC 5322. This differs from the <code>from_mailbox</code> field, which shows the message author. The sender mailbox field is most commonly used when multiple addresses appear in the <code> from_mailboxes </code> field, or when the transmitting system is different from the message author (such as when sending on behalf of someone else).
--     * Slot: size Description: The size in bytes of the email, including attachments.
--     * Slot: smtp_from Description: The value of the SMTP MAIL FROM command.
--     * Slot: subject Description: The email header Subject value, as defined by RFC 5322.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the email thread.
--     * Slot: data_classification_id Description: The Data Classification object includes information about data classification levels and data category types.
-- # Class: OcsfEmailAuth Description: The Email Authentication object describes the Sender Policy Framework (SPF), DomainKeys Identified Mail (DKIM) and Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting and Conformance (DMARC) attributes of an email.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: dkim Description: The DomainKeys Identified Mail (DKIM) status of the email.
--     * Slot: dkim_domain Description: The DomainKeys Identified Mail (DKIM) signing domain of the email.
--     * Slot: dkim_signature Description: The DomainKeys Identified Mail (DKIM) signature used by the sending/receiving system.
--     * Slot: dmarc Description: The Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting and Conformance (DMARC) status of the email.
