-- # Class: OcsfEpss Description: The Exploit Prediction Scoring System (EPSS) object describes the estimated probability a vulnerability will be exploited. EPSS is a community-driven effort to combine descriptive information about vulnerabilities (CVEs) with evidence of actual exploitation in-the-wild.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: created_time Description: The timestamp indicating when the EPSS score was calculated.
--     * Slot: created_time_dt Description: The timestamp indicating when the EPSS score was calculated.
--     * Slot: percentile Description: The EPSS score's percentile representing relative importance and ranking of the score in the larger EPSS dataset.
--     * Slot: score Description: The EPSS score representing the probability [0-1] of exploitation in the wild in the next 30 days (following score publication).
--     * Slot: version Description: The version of the EPSS model used to calculate the score.
-- # Class: OcsfEvidences Description: A collection of evidence artifacts associated to the activity/activities that triggered a security detection.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: data Description: Additional evidence data that is not accounted for in the specific evidence attributes.<code> Use only when absolutely necessary.</code>
--     * Slot: verdict Description: The normalized verdict of the evidence associated with the security detection.
--     * Slot: verdict_id Description: The normalized verdict (or status) ID of the evidence associated with the security detection. For example, Microsoft Graph Security Alerts contain a <code>verdict</code> enumeration for each type of <code>evidence</code> associated with the Alert. This is typically set by an automated investigation process or an analyst/investigator assigned to the finding.
--     * Slot: name Description: The naming convention or type identifier of the evidence associated with the security detection. For example, the <code>@odata.type</code> from Microsoft Graph Alerts V2 or <code>display_name</code> from CrowdStrike Falcon Incident Behaviors.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the evidence associated with the security detection. For example, the <code>activity_id</code> from CrowdStrike Falcon Alerts or <code>behavior_id</code> from CrowdStrike Falcon Incident Behaviors.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
--     * Slot: OcsfComplianceFinding_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfDetectionFinding_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: actor_id Description: Describes details about the user/role/process that was the source of the activity that triggered the detection.
--     * Slot: ai_agent_id Description: Describes details about an AI agent associated with the activity that triggered the detection, whether the agent performed the activity, was its target, or was otherwise involved. Use a separate <code>evidences</code> entry per agent when a finding involves more than one, such as an agent spawning a sub-agent in violation of usage policy.
--     * Slot: api_id Description: Describes details about the API call associated to the activity that triggered the detection.
--     * Slot: connection_info_id Description: Describes details about the network connection associated to the activity that triggered the detection.
--     * Slot: container_id Description: Describes details about the container associated to the activity that triggered the detection.
--     * Slot: database_id Description: Describes details about the database associated to the activity that triggered the detection.
--     * Slot: databucket_id Description: Describes details about the databucket associated to the activity that triggered the detection.
--     * Slot: device_id Description: An addressable device, computer system or host associated to the activity that triggered the detection.
--     * Slot: dst_endpoint_id Description: Describes details about the destination of the network activity that triggered the detection.
--     * Slot: email_id Description: The email object associated to the activity that triggered the detection.
--     * Slot: file_id Description: Describes details about the file associated to the activity that triggered the detection.
--     * Slot: http_request_id Description: Describes details about the http request associated to the activity that triggered the detection.
--     * Slot: http_response_id Description: Describes details about the http response associated to the activity that triggered the detection.
--     * Slot: job_id Description: Describes details about the scheduled job that was associated with the activity that triggered the detection.
--     * Slot: process_id Description: Describes details about the process associated to the activity that triggered the detection.
--     * Slot: query_id Description: Describes details about the DNS query associated to the activity that triggered the detection.
--     * Slot: reg_key_id Description: Describes details about the registry key that triggered the detection.
--     * Slot: reg_value_id Description: Describes details about the registry value that triggered the detection.
--     * Slot: script_id Description: Describes details about the script that was associated with the activity that triggered the detection.
--     * Slot: src_endpoint_id Description: Describes details about the source of the network activity that triggered the detection.
--     * Slot: tls_id Description: Describes details about the Transport Layer Security (TLS) activity that triggered the detection.
--     * Slot: url_id Description: The URL object that pertains to the event or object associated to the activity that triggered the detection.
--     * Slot: user_id Description: Describes details about the user that was the target or somehow else associated with the activity that triggered the detection.
--     * Slot: win_service_id Description: Describes details about the Windows service that triggered the detection.
-- # Class: OcsfExtension Description: The OCSF Schema Extension object provides detailed information about the schema extension used to construct the event. The schema extensions are registered in the <code>extensions.md</code> file.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: version Description: The schema extension version. For example: <code>1.0.0-alpha.2</code>.
--     * Slot: name Description: The schema extension name. For example: <code>dev</code>.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The schema extension unique identifier. For example: <code>999</code>.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
--     * Slot: OcsfMetadata_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
-- # Class: OcsfFeature Description: The Feature object provides information about the software product feature that generated a specific event. It encompasses details related to the capabilities, components, user interface (UI) design, and performance upgrades associated with the feature.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: version Description: The version of the feature.
--     * Slot: name Description: The name of the feature.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the feature.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
-- # Class: OcsfFile Description: The File object represents the metadata associated with a file stored in a computer system. It encompasses information about the file itself, including its attributes, properties, and organizational details.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: accessed_time Description: The time when the file was last accessed.
--     * Slot: accessed_time_dt Description: The time when the file was last accessed.
--     * Slot: attributes Description: The bitmask value that represents the file attributes.
--     * Slot: company_name Description: The name of the company that published the file. For example: <code>Microsoft Corporation</code>.
--     * Slot: confidentiality Description: The file content confidentiality, normalized to the confidentiality_id value. In the case of 'Other', it is defined by the event source.
--     * Slot: confidentiality_id Description: The normalized identifier of the file content confidentiality indicator.
--     * Slot: created_time Description: The time when the file was created.
--     * Slot: created_time_dt Description: The time when the file was created.
--     * Slot: desc Description: The description of the file, as returned by file system. For example: the description as returned by the Unix file command or the Windows file type.
--     * Slot: drive_type Description: The drive type, normalized to the caption of the <code>drive_type_id</code> value. In the case of <code>Other</code>, it is defined by the source.
--     * Slot: drive_type_id Description: Identifies the type of a disk drive, i.e. fixed, removable, etc.
--     * Slot: ext Description: The extension of the file, excluding the leading dot. For example: <code>exe</code> from <code>svchost.exe</code>, or <code>gz</code> from <code>export.tar.gz</code>.
--     * Slot: internal_name Description: The name of the file as identified within the file itself. This contrasts with the name by which the file is known on disk. Where available, the internal name is widely used by security practitioners and detection content because the on-disk file name is not reliable. On the Windows OS, most PE files contain a <code>VERSIONINFO</code> resource from which the internal name can be obtained. On macOS, binaries can optionally embed a copy of the application's <code>Info.plist</code> file which in turn contains the name of the executable.
--     * Slot: is_deleted Description: Indicates if the file was deleted from the filesystem.
--     * Slot: is_encrypted Description: Indicates if the file is encrypted.
--     * Slot: is_public Description: Indicates if the file is publicly accessible. For example in an object's public access in AWS S3
--     * Slot: is_readonly Description: Indicates that the file cannot be modified.
--     * Slot: is_system Description: The indication of whether the object is part of the operating system.
--     * Slot: mime_type Description: The Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (MIME) type of the file, if applicable.
--     * Slot: modified_time Description: The time when the file was last modified.
--     * Slot: modified_time_dt Description: The time when the file was last modified.
--     * Slot: parent_folder Description: The parent folder in which the file resides. For example: <code>c:\windows\system32</code>
--     * Slot: path Description: The full path to the file. For example: <code>c:\windows\system32\svchost.exe</code>.
--     * Slot: security_descriptor Description: The object security descriptor.
--     * Slot: size Description: The size of data, in bytes.
--     * Slot: storage_class Description: The storage class of the file. For example in AWS S3: <code>STANDARD, STANDARD_IA, GLACIER</code>.
--     * Slot: type Description: The file type.
--     * Slot: type_id Description: The file type ID. Note the distinction between a <code>Regular File</code> and an <code>Executable File</code>. If the distinction is not known, or not indicated by the log, use <code>Regular File</code>. In this case, it should not be assumed that a Regular File is not executable.
--     * Slot: uri Description: The file URI, such as those reporting by static analysis tools. E.g., <code>file:///C:/dev/sarif/sarif-tutorials/samples/Introduction/simple-example.js</code>
--     * Slot: version Description: The file version. For example: <code>8.0.7601.17514</code>.
--     * Slot: volume Description: The volume on the storage device where the file is located.
--     * Slot: name Description: The name of the file. For example: <code>svchost.exe</code>
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the file as defined by the storage system, such the file system file ID.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
--     * Slot: OcsfEmail_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfMalware_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: accessor_id Description: The name of the user who last accessed the object.
--     * Slot: creator_id Description: The user that created the file.
--     * Slot: download_info_id Description: Information pertaining to a downloaded file.
--     * Slot: encryption_details_id Description: The encryption details of the file. Should be populated if the file is encrypted.
--     * Slot: modifier_id Description: The user that last modified the file.
--     * Slot: owner_id Description: The user that owns the file/object.
--     * Slot: product_id Description: The product that created or installed the file.
--     * Slot: signature_id Description: The digital signature of the file.
--     * Slot: url_id Description: The URL of the file, when applicable.
--     * Slot: xattributes_id Description: An unordered collection of zero or more name/value pairs where each pair represents a file or folder extended attribute.<p>For example: Windows alternate data stream attributes (ADS stream name, ADS size, etc.), user-defined or application-defined attributes, ACL, owner, primary group, etc. Examples from DCS: </p><ul><li><strong>ads_name</strong></li><li><strong>ads_size</strong></li><li><strong>dacl</strong></li><li><strong>owner</strong></li><li><strong>primary_group</strong></li><li><strong>link_name</strong> - name of the link associated to the file.</li><li><strong>hard_link_count</strong> - the number of links that are associated to the file.</li></ul>
--     * Slot: data_classification_id Description: The Data Classification object includes information about data classification levels and data category types.
-- # Class: OcsfFinding Description: The Finding object describes metadata related to a security finding generated by a security tool or system.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: created_time Description: The time when the finding was created.
--     * Slot: created_time_dt Description: The time when the finding was created.
--     * Slot: desc Description: The description of the reported finding.
--     * Slot: first_seen_time Description: The time when the finding was first observed.
--     * Slot: first_seen_time_dt Description: The time when the finding was first observed.
--     * Slot: last_seen_time Description: The time when the finding was most recently observed.
--     * Slot: last_seen_time_dt Description: The time when the finding was most recently observed.
--     * Slot: modified_time Description: The time when the finding was last modified.
--     * Slot: modified_time_dt Description: The time when the finding was last modified.
--     * Slot: product_uid Description: The unique identifier of the product that reported the finding.
--     * Slot: src_url Description: The URL pointing to the source of the finding.
--     * Slot: supporting_data Description: Additional data supporting a finding as provided by security tool
--     * Slot: title Description: A title or a brief phrase summarizing the reported finding.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the reported finding.
--     * Slot: product_id Description: Details about the product that reported the finding.
--     * Slot: remediation_id Description: Describes the recommended remediation steps to address identified issue(s).
-- # Class: OcsfFindingInfo Description: The Finding Information object describes metadata related to a security finding generated by a security tool or system.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: created_time Description: The time when the finding was created.
--     * Slot: created_time_dt Description: The time when the finding was created.
--     * Slot: desc Description: The description of the reported finding.
--     * Slot: first_seen_time Description: The time when the finding was first observed. e.g. The time when a vulnerability was first observed. <p>It can differ from the <code>created_time</code> timestamp, which reflects the time this finding was created.</p>
--     * Slot: first_seen_time_dt Description: The time when the finding was first observed. e.g. The time when a vulnerability was first observed. <p>It can differ from the <code>created_time</code> timestamp, which reflects the time this finding was created.</p>
--     * Slot: last_seen_time Description: The time when the finding was most recently observed. e.g. The time when a vulnerability was most recently observed. <p>It can differ from the <code>modified_time</code> timestamp, which reflects the time this finding was last modified.</p>
--     * Slot: last_seen_time_dt Description: The time when the finding was most recently observed. e.g. The time when a vulnerability was most recently observed. <p>It can differ from the <code>modified_time</code> timestamp, which reflects the time this finding was last modified.</p>
--     * Slot: modified_time Description: The time when the finding was last modified.
--     * Slot: modified_time_dt Description: The time when the finding was last modified.
--     * Slot: product_uid Description: The unique identifier of the product that reported the finding.
--     * Slot: related_events_count Description: Number of related events or findings.
--     * Slot: src_url Description: The URL pointing to the source of the finding.
--     * Slot: title Description: A title or a brief phrase summarizing the reported finding.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the reported finding.
--     * Slot: uid_alt Description: The alternative unique identifier of the reported finding.
--     * Slot: OcsfIncidentFinding_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: analytic_id Description: The analytic technique used to analyze and derive insights from the data or information that led to the finding or conclusion.
--     * Slot: attack_graph_id Description: An Attack Graph describes possible routes an attacker could take through an environment. It describes relationships between resources and their findings, such as malware detections, vulnerabilities, misconfigurations, and other security actions.
--     * Slot: product_id Description: Details about the product that reported the finding.
-- # Class: OcsfFingerprint Description: The Fingerprint object provides detailed information about a fingerprint, which is a compact representation of data used to identify a longer piece of information, such as a public key, file content, or application implementation. It contains the algorithm or scheme and value of the fingerprint, enabling efficient and reliable identification of the associated data.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: algorithm Description: The algorithm or scheme used to create the fingerprint, normalized to the caption of <code>algorithm_id</code>. In the case of <code>Other</code>, it is defined by the event source.
--     * Slot: algorithm_id Description: The identifier of the normalized algorithm or scheme, which was used to create the fingerprint.
--     * Slot: encoding Description: The encoding of the <code>value</code> attribute, normalized to the caption of <code>encoding_id</code>. In the case of <code>Other</code>, it is defined by the event source.
--     * Slot: encoding_id Description: The normalized identifier of the encoding used to represent the fingerprint bytes as the string in <code>value</code>. A verifier must decode <code>value</code> using this encoding to recover the raw hash bytes.
--     * Slot: serialization Description: The canonical serialization scheme used to produce the deterministic byte sequence that was fingerprinted, normalized to the caption of <code>serialization_id</code>. In the case of <code>Other</code>, it is defined by the event source.
--     * Slot: serialization_id Description: The identifier of the normalized canonical serialization scheme used to produce the deterministic byte sequence that was fingerprinted. A verifier must apply the same scheme to reproduce the fingerprinted input. Use <code>Flat</code> where the fingerprinted data is an opaque byte sequence, such as file content, to which no canonical serialization was applied. Distinct from <code>algorithm_id</code>, which identifies how the resulting bytes were hashed.
--     * Slot: value Description: The fingerprint value.<p><b>Note:</b> This uses type <code>file_hash_t</code> (&quot;Hash&quot;), which has been generalized for all fingerprints but retains the same name and caption for backwards compatibility.</p>
--     * Slot: OcsfCertificate_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfFile_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfNetworkEndpoint_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfNetworkProxy_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfScript_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
-- # Class: OcsfRule Description: The Rule object describes characteristics of a rule associated with a policy or an event.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: category Description: The rule category.
--     * Slot: desc Description: The description of the rule that generated the event.
--     * Slot: type Description: The rule type.
--     * Slot: version Description: The rule version. For example: <code>1.1</code>.
--     * Slot: name Description: The name of the rule that generated the event.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the rule that generated the event.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
-- # Class: OcsfFirewallRule Description: The Firewall Rule object represents a specific rule within a firewall policy or event. It contains information about a rule's configuration, properties, and associated actions that define how network traffic is handled by the firewall.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: condition Description: The rule trigger condition for the rule. For example: SQL_INJECTION.
--     * Slot: duration Description: The rule response time duration, usually used for challenge completion time.
--     * Slot: match_location Description: The location of the matched data in the source which resulted in the triggered firewall rule. For example: HEADER.
--     * Slot: rate_limit Description: The rate limit for a rate-based rule.
--     * Slot: sensitivity Description: The sensitivity of the firewall rule in the matched event. For example: HIGH.
--     * Slot: category Description: The rule category.
--     * Slot: desc Description: The description of the rule that generated the event.
--     * Slot: type Description: The rule type.
--     * Slot: version Description: The rule version. For example: <code>1.1</code>.
--     * Slot: name Description: The name of the rule that generated the event.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the rule that generated the event.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
-- # Class: OcsfFunctionInvocation Description: The Function Invocation object provides details regarding the invocation of a function.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: error Description: The error indication returned from the function. This may differ from the return value (e.g. when <code>errno</code> is used).
--     * Slot: return_value Description: The value returned from a function.
-- # Class: OcsfGpuInfo Description: The GPU information object contains attributes describing graphical processing hardware.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: bus_type Description: The attachment bus or interface standard, normalized to the caption of the bus_type_id value. In the case of 'Other', it is defined by the event source.
--     * Slot: bus_type_id Description: The normalized identifier of the attachment bus or interface standard.
--     * Slot: cores Description: The number of processing cores or compute units for the component.
--     * Slot: model Description: The model name of the GPU. For example: <code>GeForce RTX A6000</code>, <code>Radeon PRO W7900</code>, or <code>Intel Data Center GPU Max 1550</code>.
--     * Slot: vendor_name Description: The name of the vendor of the GPU. For example: <code>NVIDIA</code>, <code>AMD</code>, or <code>Intel</code>.
--     * Slot: vram_mode Description: The video memory attachment mode, indicating how the VRAM hardware is integrated with the system (e.g., shared or dedicated), normalized to the caption of the vram_mode_id value. For 'Other', the exact attachment mode is defined by the event source.
--     * Slot: vram_mode_id Description: The normalized identifier of the video memory attachment mode.
--     * Slot: vram_size Description: The total amount of installed video RAM.
--     * Slot: OcsfDeviceHwInfo_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
-- # Class: OcsfGraph Description: A graph data structure representation with nodes and edges.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: desc Description: The graph description - provides additional details about the graph's purpose and contents.
--     * Slot: is_directed Description: Indicates if the graph is directed (<code>true</code>) or undirected (<code>false</code>).
--     * Slot: query_language Description: The graph query language, normalized to the caption of the <code>query_language_id</code> value.
--     * Slot: query_language_id Description: The normalized identifier of a graph query language that can be used to interact with the graph.
--     * Slot: type Description: The graph type. Typically useful to represent the specific type of graph that is used.
--     * Slot: name Description: The graph name - a human readable identifier for the graph.
--     * Slot: uid Description: Unique identifier of the graph - a unique ID to reference this specific graph.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
-- # Class: OcsfGroup Description: The Group object represents a collection or association of entities, such as users, policies, or devices. It serves as a logical grouping mechanism to organize and manage entities with similar characteristics or permissions within a system or organization, including but not limited to purposes of access control.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: desc Description: The group description.
--     * Slot: domain Description: The domain where the group is defined. For example: the LDAP or Active Directory domain.
--     * Slot: type Description: The type of the group.
--     * Slot: uid_alt Description: The alternate unique identifier.
--     * Slot: name Description: The group name.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the group. For example, for Windows events this is the security identifier (SID) of the group. Another example, pool id or desktop id that the device belongs to.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
--     * Slot: OcsfDatabase_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfDatabucket_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfDevice_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfTable_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfUser_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfAuthorizeSession_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
-- # Class: OcsfHassh Description: The HASSH object contains SSH network fingerprinting values for specific client/server implementations. It provides a standardized way of identifying and categorizing SSH connections based on their unique characteristics and behavior.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: algorithm Description: The concatenation of key exchange, encryption, authentication and compression algorithms (separated by ';'). NOTE: This is not the underlying algorithm for the hash implementation.
--     * Slot: fingerprint_id Description: The hash of the key exchange, encryption, authentication and compression algorithms.
-- # Class: OcsfHttpCookie Description: The HTTP Cookie object, also known as a web cookie or browser cookie, contains details and values pertaining to a small piece of data that a server sends to a user's web browser. This data is then stored by the browser and sent back to the server with subsequent requests, allowing the server to remember and track certain information about the user's browsing session or preferences.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: domain Description: The domain name for the server from which the http_cookie is served.
--     * Slot: expiration_time Description: The expiration time of the HTTP cookie.
--     * Slot: expiration_time_dt Description: The expiration time of the HTTP cookie.
--     * Slot: http_only Description: A cookie attribute to make it inaccessible via JavaScript
--     * Slot: is_http_only Description: This attribute prevents the cookie from being accessed via JavaScript.
--     * Slot: is_secure Description: The cookie attribute indicates that cookies are sent to the server only when the request is encrypted using the HTTPS protocol.
--     * Slot: name Description: The HTTP cookie name.
--     * Slot: path Description: The path of the HTTP cookie.
--     * Slot: samesite Description: The cookie attribute that lets servers specify whether/when cookies are sent with cross-site requests. Values are: Strict, Lax or None
--     * Slot: secure Description: The cookie attribute to only send cookies to the server with an encrypted request over the HTTPS protocol.
--     * Slot: value Description: The HTTP cookie value.
--     * Slot: OcsfHttpActivity_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
-- # Class: OcsfHttpHeader Description: The HTTP Header object represents the headers sent in an HTTP request or response. HTTP headers are key-value pairs that convey additional information about the HTTP message, including details about the content, caching, authentication, encoding, and other aspects of the communication.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: name Description: The name of the HTTP header.
--     * Slot: value Description: The value of the HTTP header.
--     * Slot: OcsfEmail_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfHttpRequest_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfHttpResponse_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
-- # Class: OcsfHttpRequest Description: The HTTP Request object represents the attributes of a request made to a web server. It encapsulates the details and metadata associated with an HTTP request, including the request method, headers, URL, query parameters, body content, and other relevant information.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: args Description: The arguments sent along with the HTTP request.
--     * Slot: body_length Description: The actual length of the HTTP request body, in number of bytes, independent of a potentially existing Content-Length header.
--     * Slot: http_method Description: The HTTP request method indicates the desired action to be performed for a given resource.
--     * Slot: length Description: The length of the entire HTTP request, in number of bytes.
--     * Slot: referrer Description: The request header that identifies the address of the previous web page, which is linked to the current web page or resource being requested.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the http request.
--     * Slot: user_agent Description: The request header that identifies the operating system and web browser.
--     * Slot: version Description: The Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP) version.
--     * Slot: url_id Description: The URL object that pertains to the request.
-- # Class: OcsfHttpResponse Description: The HTTP Response object contains detailed information about the response sent from a web server to the requester. It encompasses attributes and metadata that describe the response status, headers, body content, and other relevant information.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: body_length Description: The actual length of the HTTP response body, in number of bytes, independent of a potentially existing Content-Length header.
--     * Slot: code Description: The Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP) status code returned from the web server to the client. For example, 200.
--     * Slot: content_type Description: The HTTP request header that identifies the original media type of the resource (prior to any content encoding applied for sending).
--     * Slot: latency Description: The HTTP response latency measured in milliseconds.
--     * Slot: length Description: The length of the entire HTTP response, in number of bytes.
--     * Slot: message Description: The HTTP status code and reason phrase returned from the server.
--     * Slot: status Description: The response status. For example: A successful HTTP status of 'OK' which corresponds to a code of 200.
-- # Class: OcsfIamRole Description: The IAM Role object describes permission characteristics (or privileges) associated with a user or group of users as security principals against one or more resources. In some cases only the name or ID of the role is known hence other attributes are optional. In some systems a user or group of users can have multiple roles, imparting the union of privileges to the principal. In some systems the role can serve as a temporary security principal without its own credentials on behalf of the actual security principal.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: uid_alt Description: The alternate user identifier. For example, the Active Directory role GUID or the associated LDAP group DN.
--     * Slot: name Description: The role name. For example, <code>Power Users</code>.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique role identifier. For example, the Windows role SID, or AWS role ARN.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
--     * Slot: OcsfAuthorizeSession_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfGroupManagement_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfUserManagement_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: account_id Description: The account associated with the role. For example, can be a cross-account role, different from the user or group account, granting access within this account.
--     * Slot: session_id Description: The session where the role is active. Some roles may be temporary and expire when the session expires.
-- # Class: OcsfIdentityActivityMetrics Description: The Identity Activity Metrics object captures usage patterns, authentication activity, credential usage and other metrics for identities across cloud and on-premises environments. Example identities include AWS IAM Users, Roles, Azure AD Principals, GCP Service Accounts, on-premises Active Directory accounts.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: first_seen_time Description: The timestamp when this identity was first observed or created in the system. This helps establish the identity's age and lifecycle stage for risk assessment.
--     * Slot: first_seen_time_dt Description: The timestamp when this identity was first observed or created in the system. This helps establish the identity's age and lifecycle stage for risk assessment.
--     * Slot: last_authentication_time Description: The timestamp when this identity last successfully authenticated to any system or service. This differs from <code>last_seen_time</code> as it specifically tracks authentication events rather than all activities.
--     * Slot: last_authentication_time_dt Description: The timestamp when this identity last successfully authenticated to any system or service. This differs from <code>last_seen_time</code> as it specifically tracks authentication events rather than all activities.
--     * Slot: last_seen_time Description: The timestamp of the most recent activity performed by this identity, including authentication, resource access, or API calls. This is the most comprehensive indicator of identity usage recency.
--     * Slot: last_seen_time_dt Description: The timestamp of the most recent activity performed by this identity, including authentication, resource access, or API calls. This is the most comprehensive indicator of identity usage recency.
--     * Slot: password_last_used_time Description: The timestamp when password-based authentication was last used by this identity. This helps distinguish between password and other authentication methods (MFA, SSO, certificates) and identify password-specific usage patterns.
--     * Slot: password_last_used_time_dt Description: The timestamp when password-based authentication was last used by this identity. This helps distinguish between password and other authentication methods (MFA, SSO, certificates) and identify password-specific usage patterns.
-- # Class: OcsfIdp Description: The Identity Provider object contains detailed information about a provider responsible for creating, maintaining, and managing identity information while offering authentication services to applications. An Identity Provider (IdP) serves as a trusted authority that verifies the identity of users and issues authentication tokens or assertions to enable secure access to applications or services.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: domain Description: The primary domain associated with the Identity Provider.
--     * Slot: has_mfa Description: The Identity Provider enforces Multi Factor Authentication (MFA).
--     * Slot: issuer Description: The unique identifier (often a URL) used by the Identity Provider as its issuer.
--     * Slot: protocol_name Description: The supported protocol of the Identity Provider. E.g., <code>SAML</code>, <code>OIDC</code>, or <code>OAuth2</code>.
--     * Slot: state Description: The configuration state of the Identity Provider, normalized to the caption of the <code>state_id</code> value. In the case of <code>Other</code>, it is defined by the event source.
--     * Slot: state_id Description: The normalized state ID of the Identity Provider to reflect its configuration or activation status.
--     * Slot: tenant_uid Description: The tenant ID associated with the Identity Provider.
--     * Slot: url_string Description: The URL for accessing the configuration or metadata of the Identity Provider.
--     * Slot: name Description: The name of the Identity Provider.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the Identity Provider.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
--     * Slot: fingerprint_id Description: The fingerprint of the X.509 certificate used by the Identity Provider.
--     * Slot: scim_id Description: The System for Cross-domain Identity Management (SCIM) resource object as defined in RFC 7634 provides a structured set of attributes related to SCIM protocols used for identity provisioning and management across cloud-based platforms. It standardizes user and group provisioning details, enabling identity synchronization and lifecycle management with compatible Identity Providers (IdPs) and applications.
--     * Slot: sso_id Description: The Single Sign-On (SSO) object provides a structure for normalizing SSO attributes, configuration, and/or settings from Identity Providers.
-- # Class: OcsfImage Description: The Image object provides a description of a specific Virtual Machine (VM) or Container image.
--     * Slot: id
