-- # Class: OcsfScan Description: The Scan object describes characteristics of a proactive scan.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: type Description: The type of scan.
--     * Slot: type_id Description: The type id of the scan.
--     * Slot: name Description: The administrator-supplied or application-generated name of the scan. For example: "Home office weekly user database scan", "Scan folders for viruses", "Full system virus scan"
--     * Slot: uid Description: The application-defined unique identifier assigned to an instance of a scan.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
-- # Class: OcsfMalwareScanInfo Description: The malware scan information object describes characteristics, metadata of a malware scanning job.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: end_time Description: The timestamp indicating when the scan job completed execution.
--     * Slot: end_time_dt Description: The timestamp indicating when the scan job completed execution.
--     * Slot: num_files Description: The total number of files analyzed during the scan.
--     * Slot: num_infected Description: The total number of files identified as infected with malware during the scan.
--     * Slot: num_volumes Description: The total number of storage volumes examined during the malware scan.
--     * Slot: size Description: The total size in bytes of all files that were scanned.
--     * Slot: start_time Description: The timestamp indicating when the scan job began execution.
--     * Slot: start_time_dt Description: The timestamp indicating when the scan job began execution.
--     * Slot: unique_malware_count Description: The number of unique malware detected across all infected files.
--     * Slot: type Description: The type of scan.
--     * Slot: type_id Description: The type id of the scan.
--     * Slot: name Description: The administrator-supplied or application-generated name of the scan. For example: "Home office weekly user database scan", "Scan folders for viruses", "Full system virus scan"
--     * Slot: uid Description: The application-defined unique identifier assigned to an instance of a scan.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
-- # Class: OcsfManagedEntity Description: The Managed Entity object describes the type and version of an entity, such as a user, device, or policy.  For types in the <code>type_id</code> enum list, an associated attribute should be populated.  If the type of entity is not in the <code>type_id</code> list, information can be put into the <code>data</code> attribute, <code>type_id</code> should be 'Other' and the <code>type</code> attribute should label the entity type.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: data Description: The managed entity content as a JSON object.
--     * Slot: type Description: The managed entity type. For example: <code>Policy</code>, <code>User</code>, <code>Organization</code>, <code>Device</code>.
--     * Slot: type_id Description: The type of the Managed Entity. It is recommended to also populate the <code>type</code> attribute with the associated label, or the source specific name if <code>Other</code>.
--     * Slot: version Description: The version of the managed entity. For example: <code>1.2.3</code>.
--     * Slot: name Description: The name of the managed entity. It should match the name of the specific entity object's name if populated, or the name of the managed entity if the <code>type_id</code> is 'Other'.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The identifier of the managed entity. It should match the <code>uid</code> of the specific entity's object UID if populated, or the source specific ID if the <code>type_id</code> is 'Other'.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
--     * Slot: device_id Description: An addressable device, computer system or host.
--     * Slot: email_id Description: The email object.
--     * Slot: group_id Description: The group object associated with an entity such as user, policy, or rule.
--     * Slot: location_id Description: The detailed geographical location usually associated with an IP address.
--     * Slot: org_id Description: The Organization object containing details about the managed organizational entity. This object includes properties such as the organization name, unique identifier, type, and other organizational metadata. This attribute should be populated when <code>type_id</code> is <code>4</code> (Organization).
--     * Slot: policy_id Description: Describes details of a managed policy.
--     * Slot: user_id Description: The user that pertains to the event or object.
-- # Class: OcsfMessageContext Description: Communication context for AI system interactions including protocols, roles, clients, and session information for MCP and other AI communication systems.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: ai_role Description: The normalized caption of the <code>ai_role_id</code>.
--     * Slot: ai_role_id Description: Specifies the functional role of the AI within the context of this message, such as retrieving information, assisting reasoning, executing a tool, or generating content.
--     * Slot: completion_tokens Description: Number of tokens in the model's response/completion for this message.
--     * Slot: prompt_text Description: The text of the input prompt provided to the model for this message. This is the verbatim instruction, query, or context supplied to the AI system that drove the operation.
--     * Slot: prompt_tokens Description: Number of tokens in the input prompt for this message.
--     * Slot: response_text Description: The text of the model's response generated for this message. For agent-mediated operations, this is the content returned by the assistant, tool, or agent.
--     * Slot: total_tokens Description: Total number of tokens used for this message (prompt + completion).
--     * Slot: name Description: The name or identifier of the message context. In AI systems, this could be the conversation ID, session name, thread identifier, or interaction name (e.g., 'user-session-123', 'conversation-abc', 'chat-thread-456').
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the message context. This could be a session ID, conversation ID, or other unique identifier that allows correlation of messages within the same context.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
--     * Slot: application_id Description: The initiating client application. In AI systems, this represents the client-side application or framework that initiates requests (e.g., LangChain application, web browser, mobile app, SDK implementation).
--     * Slot: service_id Description: The server or service handling the request. In AI systems, this represents the AI service, API endpoint, or agent that processes and responds to requests (e.g., OpenAI API service, Claude API service, internal AI model service).
-- # Class: OcsfMetadata Description: The Metadata object describes the metadata associated with the event.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: correlation_uid Description: A unique identifier used to correlate this OCSF event with other related OCSF events, distinct from the event's <code>uid</code> value. This enables linking multiple OCSF events that are part of the same activity, transaction, or security incident across different systems or time periods.
--     * Slot: event_code Description: The identifier of the original event. For example the numerical Windows Event Code or Cisco syslog code.
--     * Slot: is_truncated Description: Indicates whether the OCSF event data has been truncated due to size limitations. When <code>true</code>, some event data may have been omitted to fit within system constraints.
--     * Slot: log_format Description: The format of data in the log where the data originated. For example CSV, XML, Windows Multiline, JSON, syslog or Cisco Log Schema.
--     * Slot: log_level Description: The level at which an event was logged. This can be log provider specific. For example the audit level.
--     * Slot: log_name Description: The event log name, typically for the consumer of the event. For example, the storage bucket name, SIEM repository index name, etc.
--     * Slot: log_provider Description: The logging provider or logging service that logged the event. For example AWS CloudWatch or Splunk.
--     * Slot: log_source Description: The log system or component where the data originated. For example, a file path, syslog server name or a Windows hostname and logging subsystem such as Security.
--     * Slot: log_version Description: The event log schema version of the original event. For example the syslog version or the Cisco Log Schema version
--     * Slot: logged_time Description: The ultimate logged time in the event pipeline - when the event reached its final destination (e.g., SIEM, data lake). If <code>loggers</code> is populated, this should match the <code>logged_time</code> of the last entry in the <code>loggers</code> array. This is distinct from <code>time</code> (when the activity occurred) and <code>processed_time</code> (when an intermediate system processed the event).
--     * Slot: logged_time_dt Description: The ultimate logged time in the event pipeline - when the event reached its final destination (e.g., SIEM, data lake). If <code>loggers</code> is populated, this should match the <code>logged_time</code> of the last entry in the <code>loggers</code> array. This is distinct from <code>time</code> (when the activity occurred) and <code>processed_time</code> (when an intermediate system processed the event).
--     * Slot: modified_time Description: The time when the event was last modified or enriched.
--     * Slot: modified_time_dt Description: The time when the event was last modified or enriched.
--     * Slot: original_event_uid Description: The unique identifier assigned to the event in its original logging system before transformation to OCSF format. This field preserves the source system's native event identifier, enabling traceability back to the raw log entry. For example, a Windows Event Record ID, a syslog message ID, a Splunk _cd value, or a database transaction log sequence number.
--     * Slot: original_time Description: The original event time as reported by the event source, preserved as a pass-through string in its native format (e.g., Syslog timestamp, Windows event time). This is not normalized to <code>timestamp_t</code> - the normalized equivalent is the base event <code>time</code> attribute. Omit if the event is generated rather than collected via logs.
--     * Slot: processed_time Description: The time when the event was processed by an intermediate system (e.g., ETL pipeline, event processor) before reaching its final destination. Can be used with <code>logged_time</code> to calculate queuing duration via <code>total_queued_duration</code>.
--     * Slot: processed_time_dt Description: The time when the event was processed by an intermediate system (e.g., ETL pipeline, event processor) before reaching its final destination. Can be used with <code>logged_time</code> to calculate queuing duration via <code>total_queued_duration</code>.
--     * Slot: sequence Description: Sequence number of the event. The sequence number is a value available in some events, to make the exact ordering of events unambiguous, regardless of the event time precision.
--     * Slot: source Description: The source of the event or finding. This can be any distinguishing name for the logical origin of the data — for example, 'CloudTrail Events', or a use case like 'Attack Simulations' or 'Vulnerability Scans'.
--     * Slot: tenant_uid Description: The unique tenant identifier.
--     * Slot: transmit_time Description: The time when the event was transmitted from the logging device to it's next destination.
--     * Slot: transmit_time_dt Description: The time when the event was transmitted from the logging device to it's next destination.
--     * Slot: type Description: The type of the event or finding as a subset of the <code>source</code> of the event. This can be any distinguishing characteristic of the data. For example 'Management Events' or 'Device Penetration Test'.
--     * Slot: uid Description: A unique identifier assigned to the OCSF event. This ID is specific to the OCSF event itself and is distinct from the original event identifier in the source system (see <code>original_event_uid</code>).
--     * Slot: untruncated_size Description: The original size of the OCSF event data in kilobytes before any truncation occurred. This field is typically populated when <code>is_truncated</code> is <code>true</code> to indicate the full size of the original event.
--     * Slot: version Description: The version of the OCSF schema, using Semantic Versioning Specification (SemVer). For example: <code>1.0.0</code>. Event consumers use the version to determine the available event attributes.
--     * Slot: extension_id Description: The schema extension used to create the event.
--     * Slot: product_id Description: The product that reported the event.
--     * Slot: reporter_id Description: The entity from which the event or finding was first reported.
--     * Slot: total_queued_duration_id Description: The amount of time an event spent in a queue awaiting processing. In this case, the value is the difference between <code>processed_time</code> and <code>logged_time</code>. This duration is inclusive of all queues between the originator of the event and the intended long-term storage destination of the event.
--     * Slot: data_classification_id Description: The Data Classification object includes information about data classification levels and data category types.
-- # Class: OcsfMetric Description: The Metric object defines a simple name/value pair entity for a metric.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: name Description: The name of the metric.
--     * Slot: value Description: The value of the metric.
--     * Slot: OcsfCvss_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfLoadBalancer_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
-- # Class: OcsfMitigation Description: The MITRE Mitigation object describes the ATT&CK® or ATLAS™ Mitigation ID and/or name that is associated to an attack.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: src_url Description: The versioned permalink of the Mitigation. For example: <code>https://attack.mitre.org/versions/v14/mitigations/M1027</code>.
--     * Slot: name Description: The Mitigation name that is associated with the attack technique. For example: <code>Password Policies</code>, or <code>Code Signing</code>.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The Mitigation ID that is associated with the attack technique. For example: <code>M1027</code>, or <code>AML.M0013</code>.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
-- # Class: OcsfModule Description: The Module object describes the attributes of a module.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: base_address Description: The memory address where the module was loaded.
--     * Slot: function_name Description: The invoked function in the module. For load and unload events, this is the entry-point function of the module. The system calls the entry-point function whenever a process or thread loads or unloads the module.
--     * Slot: load_type Description: The load type, normalized to the caption of the load_type_id value. In the case of 'Other', it is defined by the event source.
--     * Slot: load_type_id Description: The normalized identifier for how the module was loaded in memory.
--     * Slot: start_address Description: The start address of the execution.
--     * Slot: type Description: The module type.
--     * Slot: file_id Description: The module file object.
--     * Slot: function_invocation_id Description: Details about the invocation of the function given in <code>function_name</code>.
-- # Class: OcsfNetworkConnectionInfo Description: The Network Connection Information object describes characteristics of an OSI Transport Layer communication, including TCP and UDP.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: boundary Description: The boundary of the connection, normalized to the caption of 'boundary_id'. In the case of 'Other', it is defined by the event source. <p> For cloud connections, this translates to the traffic-boundary(same VPC, through IGW, etc.). For traditional networks, this is described as Local, Internal, or External.</p>
--     * Slot: boundary_id Description: <p>The normalized identifier of the boundary of the connection. </p><p> For cloud connections, this translates to the traffic-boundary (same VPC, through IGW, etc.). For traditional networks, this is described as Local, Internal, or External.</p>
--     * Slot: community_uid Description: The Community ID of the network connection.
--     * Slot: direction Description: The direction of the initiated connection, traffic, or email, normalized to the caption of the direction_id value. In the case of 'Other', it is defined by the event source.
--     * Slot: direction_id Description: The normalized identifier of the direction of the initiated connection, traffic, or email.
--     * Slot: flag_history Description: The Connection Flag History summarizes events in a network connection. For example flags <code> ShAD </code> representing SYN, SYN/ACK, ACK and Data exchange.
--     * Slot: protocol_name Description: The IP protocol name in lowercase, as defined by the Internet Assigned Numbers Authority (IANA). For example: <code>tcp</code> or <code>udp</code>.
--     * Slot: protocol_num Description: The IP protocol number, as defined by the Internet Assigned Numbers Authority (IANA). For example: <code>6</code> for TCP and <code>17</code> for UDP.
--     * Slot: protocol_ver Description: The Internet Protocol version.
--     * Slot: protocol_ver_id Description: The Internet Protocol version identifier.
--     * Slot: tcp_flags Description: The network connection TCP header flags (i.e., control bits).
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the connection.
--     * Slot: session_id Description: The authenticated user or service session.
-- # Class: OcsfNetworkEndpoint Description: The Network Endpoint object describes characteristics of a network endpoint. These can be a source or destination of a network connection.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: isp Description: The name of the Internet Service Provider (ISP).
--     * Slot: isp_org Description: The organization name of the Internet Service Provider (ISP). This represents the parent organization or company that owns/operates the ISP. For example, Comcast Corporation would be the ISP org for Xfinity internet service. This attribute helps identify the ultimate provider when ISPs operate under different brand names.
--     * Slot: network_scope Description: Indicates whether the endpoint resides inside the customer’s network, outside on the Internet, or if its location relative to the customer’s network cannot be determined. The value is normalized to the caption of the <code>network_scope_id</code>.
--     * Slot: network_scope_id Description: The normalized identifier of the endpoint’s network scope. The normalized network scope identifier indicates whether the endpoint resides inside the customer’s network, outside on the Internet, or if its location relative to the customer’s network cannot be determined.
--     * Slot: port Description: The port used for communication within the network connection.
--     * Slot: svc_name Description: The service name in service-to-service connections. For example, AWS VPC logs the pkt-src-aws-service and pkt-dst-aws-service fields identify the connection is coming from or going to an AWS service.
--     * Slot: domain Description: The name of the domain that the endpoint belongs to or that corresponds to the endpoint.
--     * Slot: hostname Description: The fully qualified name of the endpoint.
--     * Slot: instance_uid Description: The unique identifier of a VM instance.
--     * Slot: interface_name Description: The name of the network interface (e.g. eth2).
--     * Slot: interface_uid Description: The unique identifier of the network interface.
--     * Slot: ip Description: The IP address of the endpoint, in either IPv4 or IPv6 format.
--     * Slot: mac Description: The Media Access Control (MAC) address of the endpoint.
--     * Slot: mac_vendor Description: The vendor or manufacturer of the endpoint's network interface controller (NIC), as identified from the MAC address.
--     * Slot: subnet_uid Description: The unique identifier of a virtual subnet.
--     * Slot: type Description: The network endpoint type. For example: <code>unknown</code>, <code>server</code>, <code>desktop</code>, <code>laptop</code>, <code>tablet</code>, <code>mobile</code>, <code>virtual</code>, <code>browser</code>, or <code>other</code>.
--     * Slot: type_id Description: The network endpoint type ID.
--     * Slot: vlan_uid Description: The Virtual LAN identifier.
--     * Slot: vpc_uid Description: The unique identifier of the Virtual Private Cloud (VPC).
--     * Slot: zone Description: The network zone or LAN segment.
--     * Slot: namespace_pid Description: If running under a process namespace (such as in a container), the process identifier within that process namespace.
--     * Slot: name Description: The short name of the endpoint.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the endpoint.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
--     * Slot: autonomous_system_id Description: The Autonomous System details associated with an IP address.
--     * Slot: proxy_endpoint_id Description: The network proxy information pertaining to a specific endpoint. This can be used to describe information pertaining to network address translation (NAT).
--     * Slot: hw_info_id Description: The endpoint hardware information.
--     * Slot: location_id Description: The geographical location of the endpoint.
--     * Slot: os_id Description: The endpoint operating system.
--     * Slot: owner_id Description: The identity of the service or user account that owns the endpoint or was last logged into it.
--     * Slot: pool_id Description: The pool of desktops or virtual machines to which the endpoint belongs.
--     * Slot: container_id Description: The information describing an instance of a container. A container is a prepackaged, portable system image that runs isolated on an existing system using a container runtime like containerd.
-- # Class: OcsfNetworkInterface Description: The Network Interface object describes the type and associated attributes of a physical or virtual network interface.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: hostname Description: The hostname associated with the network interface.
--     * Slot: ip Description: The IP address associated with the network interface.
--     * Slot: mac Description: The MAC address of the network interface.
--     * Slot: namespace Description: The namespace is useful in merger or acquisition situations. For example, when similar entities exist that you need to keep separate.
--     * Slot: subnet_prefix Description: The subnet prefix length determines the number of bits used to represent the network part of the IP address. The remaining bits are reserved for identifying individual hosts within that subnet.
--     * Slot: type Description: The type of network interface.
--     * Slot: type_id Description: The network interface type identifier.
--     * Slot: name Description: The name of the network interface.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier for the network interface.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
--     * Slot: OcsfDevice_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfQueryEvidence_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
