--     * Slot: OcsfUserManagement_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: group_id Description: The policy group.
-- # Class: OcsfPortInfo Description: The Port Information object describes a port and its associated protocol details.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: port Description: The port number. For example: <code>80</code>, <code>443</code>, <code>22</code>.
--     * Slot: protocol_name Description: The IP protocol name in lowercase, as defined by the Internet Assigned Numbers Authority (IANA). For example: <code>tcp</code> or <code>udp</code>.
--     * Slot: protocol_num Description: The IP protocol number, as defined by the Internet Assigned Numbers Authority (IANA). For example: <code>6</code> for TCP and <code>17</code> for UDP.
--     * Slot: OcsfNetworkInterface_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
-- # Class: OcsfPrevEvent Description: The Previous Event object references the previous event in a chain of events. Refer to specific usage.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: type_uid Description: The event type identifier of the previous event, as carried in that event's <code>type_uid</code>. It directs a consumer to the event class, and therefore the table or store, where the previous event resides.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the previous event, as carried in that event's <code>metadata.uid</code>.
--     * Slot: fingerprint_id Description: The fingerprint of the previous event, binding this reference to the previous event's content so that altering or substituting the previous event breaks the link. Refer to specific usage.
-- # Class: OcsfPrivilegeAttackInfo Description: The Privilege Attack Info object groups privileges by the potential attack they could enable. It maps specific privileges to MITRE ATT&CK techniques, helping identify security risks associated with granted permissions.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: OcsfServicePrivilegeAnalysis_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: attack_id Description: The MITRE ATT&CK technique that these privileges could enable.
-- # Class: OcsfPrivilegeInfo Description: The Privilege Info object describes information about a specific privilege, action, or permission. It captures the privilege name, type, usage status, and when it was last used.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: is_unused Description: Indicates whether the privilege is unused within the analysis timeframe.
--     * Slot: last_used_time Description: The most recent time this privilege was used.
--     * Slot: last_used_time_dt Description: The most recent time this privilege was used.
--     * Slot: name Description: The name of the privilege, action, or permission. Examples: <code>GetObject</code>, <code>CreateStoreImageTask</code> (AWS); <code>Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts/read</code> (Azure); <code>storage.objects.get</code> (GCP).
--     * Slot: type Description: The type or category of the privilege, normalized to the caption of the <code>type_id</code> value. In the case of 'Other', it is defined by the event source.
--     * Slot: type_id Description: The normalized type of the privilege.
--     * Slot: OcsfPrivilegeAttackInfo_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
-- # Class: OcsfProcessEntity Description: The Process Entity object provides critical fields for referencing a process.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: cmd_line Description: The full command line used to launch an application, service, process, or job. For example: <code>ssh user@10.0.0.10</code>. If the command line is unavailable or missing, the empty string <code>''</code> is to be used.
--     * Slot: cpid Description: A unique process identifier that can be assigned deterministically by multiple system data producers.
--     * Slot: created_time Description: The time when the process was created/started.
--     * Slot: created_time_dt Description: The time when the process was created/started.
--     * Slot: path Description: The process file path.
--     * Slot: pid Description: The process identifier, as reported by the operating system. Process ID (PID) is a number used by the operating system to uniquely identify an active process.
--     * Slot: name Description: The friendly name of the process, for example: <code>Notepad++</code>.
--     * Slot: uid Description: A unique identifier for this process assigned by the producer (tool).  Facilitates correlation of a process event with other events for that process.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
--     * Slot: OcsfProcess_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
-- # Class: OcsfProcess Description: The Process object describes a running instance of a launched program.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: integrity Description: The process integrity level, normalized to the caption of the integrity_id value. In the case of 'Other', it is defined by the event source (Windows only).
--     * Slot: integrity_id Description: The normalized identifier of the process integrity level (Windows only).
--     * Slot: ptid Description: The identifier of the process thread associated with the event, as returned by the operating system.
--     * Slot: sandbox Description: The name of the containment jail (i.e., sandbox). For example, hardened_ps, high_security_ps, oracle_ps, netsvcs_ps, or default_ps.
--     * Slot: terminated_time Description: The time when the process was terminated.
--     * Slot: terminated_time_dt Description: The time when the process was terminated.
--     * Slot: tid Description: The identifier of the thread associated with the event, as returned by the operating system.
--     * Slot: working_directory Description: The working directory of a process.
--     * Slot: namespace_pid Description: If running under a process namespace (such as in a container), the process identifier within that process namespace.
--     * Slot: auid Description: The audit user assigned at login by the audit subsystem.
--     * Slot: egid Description: The effective group under which this process is running.
--     * Slot: euid Description: The effective user under which this process is running.
--     * Slot: cmd_line Description: The full command line used to launch an application, service, process, or job. For example: <code>ssh user@10.0.0.10</code>. If the command line is unavailable or missing, the empty string <code>''</code> is to be used.
--     * Slot: cpid Description: A unique process identifier that can be assigned deterministically by multiple system data producers.
--     * Slot: created_time Description: The time when the process was created/started.
--     * Slot: created_time_dt Description: The time when the process was created/started.
--     * Slot: path Description: The process file path.
--     * Slot: pid Description: The process identifier, as reported by the operating system. Process ID (PID) is a number used by the operating system to uniquely identify an active process.
--     * Slot: name Description: The friendly name of the process, for example: <code>Notepad++</code>.
--     * Slot: uid Description: A unique identifier for this process assigned by the producer (tool).  Facilitates correlation of a process event with other events for that process.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
--     * Slot: ai_agent_id Description: The autonomous AI agent that this process is running or hosting, if applicable. Populate when the process serves as the runtime environment for an AI agent (e.g., a Python process executing a LangChain agent or hosting an MCP server). When a process hosts multiple agents and the activity cannot be attributed to one specifically, use <code>hosted_ai_agent_list</code> instead.
--     * Slot: file_id Description: The process file object.
--     * Slot: parent_process_id Description: The parent process of this process object. It is recommended to only populate this field for the top-level process object, to prevent deep nesting. Additional ancestry information can be supplied in the <code>ancestry</code> attribute.
--     * Slot: session_id Description: The user session under which this process is running.
--     * Slot: user_id Description: The user under which this process is running.
--     * Slot: xattributes_id Description: An unordered collection of zero or more name/value pairs that represent a process extended attribute.<p>For example: process security context attributes, process resource limits, container-specific attributes, or platform-specific process metadata not covered by standard OCSF attributes.</p>
--     * Slot: container_id Description: The information describing an instance of a container. A container is a prepackaged, portable system image that runs isolated on an existing system using a container runtime like containerd.
--     * Slot: group_id Description: The group under which this process is running.
-- # Class: OcsfProduct Description: The Product object describes characteristics of a software product.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: cpe_name Description: The Common Platform Enumeration (CPE) name. For example: <code>cpe:/a:apple:safari:16.2</code>.
--     * Slot: lang Description: The two letter lower case language codes, as defined by ISO 639-1. For example: <code>en</code> (English), <code>de</code> (German), or <code>fr</code> (French).
--     * Slot: path Description: The installation path of the product.
--     * Slot: url_string Description: The URL pointing towards the product.
--     * Slot: vendor_name Description: The name of the vendor of the product.
--     * Slot: version Description: The version of the product, as defined by the event source. For example: <code>2013.1.3-beta</code>.
--     * Slot: name Description: The name of the product.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the product.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
--     * Slot: feature_id Description: The feature that reported the event.
--     * Slot: data_classification_id Description: The Data Classification object includes information about data classification levels and data category types.
-- # Class: OcsfProgrammaticCredential Description: The Programmatic Credential object describes service-specific credentials used for direct API access and system integration. These credentials are typically issued by individual services or platforms for accessing their APIs and resources, focusing on credential lifecycle management and usage tracking. Examples include API keys, service account keys, client certificates, and vendor-specific access tokens.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: last_used_time Description: The timestamp when this programmatic credential was last used for authentication or API access. This helps track credential usage patterns, identify dormant credentials that may pose security risks, and support credential lifecycle management. The timestamp should reflect the most recent successful authentication or API call using this credential.
--     * Slot: last_used_time_dt Description: The timestamp when this programmatic credential was last used for authentication or API access. This helps track credential usage patterns, identify dormant credentials that may pose security risks, and support credential lifecycle management. The timestamp should reflect the most recent successful authentication or API call using this credential.
--     * Slot: type Description: The type or category of programmatic credential, normalized to the caption of the type_id value. In the case of 'Other', it is defined by the event source. Examples include 'API Key', 'Service Account Key', 'Access Token', 'Client Certificate', 'OAuth Token', 'Personal Access Token', etc.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the programmatic credential. This could be an API key ID, service account key ID, access token identifier, certificate serial number, or other unique identifier that distinguishes this credential from others. Examples: AWS Access Key ID, GCP Service Account Key ID, Azure Application ID, or OAuth2 token identifier.
--     * Slot: OcsfIamRole_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfIdentityActivityMetrics_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfUser_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfRoleManagement_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
-- # Class: OcsfQueryEvidence Description: The specific resulting evidence information that was queried or discovered. When mapping raw telemetry data users should select the appropriate child object that best matches the evidence type as defined by query_type_id.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: query_type Description: The normalized caption of query_type_id or the source-specific query type.
--     * Slot: query_type_id Description: The normalized type of system query performed against a device or system component.
--     * Slot: state Description: The state of the socket, normalized to the caption of the state_id value. In the case of 'Other', it is defined by the event source.
--     * Slot: tcp_state_id Description: The state of the TCP socket for the network connection.
--     * Slot: connection_info_id Description: The network connection information related to a Network Connection query type.
--     * Slot: file_id Description: The file that is the target of the query when query_type_id indicates a File query.
--     * Slot: folder_id Description: The folder that is the target of the query when query_type_id indicates a Folder query.
--     * Slot: group_id Description: The administrative group that is the target of the query when query_type_id indicates an Admin Group query.
--     * Slot: job_id Description: The job object that pertains to the event when query_type_id indicates a Job query.
--     * Slot: kernel_id Description: The kernel object that pertains to the event when query_type_id indicates a Kernel query.
--     * Slot: module_id Description: The module that pertains to the event when query_type_id indicates a Module query.
--     * Slot: peripheral_device_id Description: The peripheral device that triggered the event when query_type_id indicates a Peripheral Device query.
--     * Slot: process_id Description: The process that pertains to the event when query_type_id indicates a Process query.
--     * Slot: reg_key_id Description: The registry key object describes a Windows registry key.
--     * Slot: reg_value_id Description: The registry value object describes a Windows registry value.
--     * Slot: service_id Description: The service that pertains to the event when query_type_id indicates a Service query.
--     * Slot: session_id Description: The authenticated user or service session when query_type_id indicates a Session query.
--     * Slot: startup_item_id Description: The startup item object that pertains to the event when query_type_id indicates a Startup Item query.
--     * Slot: user_id Description: The user that pertains to the event when query_type_id indicates a User query.
-- # Class: OcsfQueryInfo Description: The query info object holds information related to data access within a datastore. To access, manipulate, delete, or retrieve data from a datastore, a query must be written using a specific syntax.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: bytes Description: The size of the data returned from the query.
--     * Slot: data Description: The data returned from the query execution.
--     * Slot: query_string Description: A string representing the query code being run. For example: <code>SELECT * FROM my_table</code>
--     * Slot: query_time Description: The time when the query was run.
--     * Slot: query_time_dt Description: The time when the query was run.
--     * Slot: name Description: The query name for a saved or scheduled query.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the query.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
-- # Class: OcsfRelatedEvent Description: The Related Event object describes an event or another finding related to a finding. It may or may not be an OCSF event.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: count Description: The number of times that activity in the same logical group occurred, as reported by the related Finding.
--     * Slot: created_time Description: <p>The time when the related event/finding was created.</p> If the related event/finding is in OCSF and is a Finding, then this value should be equal to <code>finding_info.created_time</code> in the corresponding Finding. If the related event/finding is in OCSF and is not a Finding, then this value should be equal to <code>time</code> in the corresponding event.
--     * Slot: created_time_dt Description: <p>The time when the related event/finding was created.</p> If the related event/finding is in OCSF and is a Finding, then this value should be equal to <code>finding_info.created_time</code> in the corresponding Finding. If the related event/finding is in OCSF and is not a Finding, then this value should be equal to <code>time</code> in the corresponding event.
--     * Slot: desc Description: A description of the related event/finding.
--     * Slot: first_seen_time Description: The time when the finding was first observed. e.g. The time when a vulnerability was first observed.<br>It can differ from the <code>created_time</code> timestamp, which reflects the time this finding was created.
--     * Slot: first_seen_time_dt Description: The time when the finding was first observed. e.g. The time when a vulnerability was first observed.<br>It can differ from the <code>created_time</code> timestamp, which reflects the time this finding was created.
--     * Slot: last_seen_time Description: The time when the finding was most recently observed. e.g. The time when a vulnerability was most recently observed.<br>It can differ from the <code>modified_time</code> timestamp, which reflects the time this finding was last modified.
--     * Slot: last_seen_time_dt Description: The time when the finding was most recently observed. e.g. The time when a vulnerability was most recently observed.<br>It can differ from the <code>modified_time</code> timestamp, which reflects the time this finding was last modified.
--     * Slot: modified_time Description: The time when the related event/finding was last modified.
--     * Slot: modified_time_dt Description: The time when the related event/finding was last modified.
--     * Slot: product_uid Description: The unique identifier of the product that reported the related event.
--     * Slot: severity Description: The event/finding severity label, normalized to the caption of the <code>severity_id</code> value. When <code>severity_id</code> is <code>99</code> (Other), this attribute <b>must</b> contain the source-specific severity label. For all other values, this should match the caption defined for that <code>severity_id</code> enum value (e.g., <code>"High"</code> for <code>severity_id: 4</code>).
--     * Slot: severity_id Description: <p>The normalized identifier of the event/finding severity.</p>The normalized severity is a measurement the effort and expense required to manage and resolve an event or incident. Smaller numerical values represent lower impact events, and larger numerical values represent higher impact events.
--     * Slot: status Description: The related event status. Should correspond to the label of the status_id (or 'Other' status value for status_id = 99) of the related event.
--     * Slot: title Description: A title or a brief phrase summarizing the related event/finding.
--     * Slot: type Description: <p>The type of the related event/finding.</p>Populate if the related event/finding is <code>NOT</code> in OCSF. If it is in OCSF, then utilize <code>type_name, type_uid</code> instead.
--     * Slot: type_name Description: The type of the related OCSF event, as defined by <code>type_uid</code>.<p>For example: <code>Process Activity: Launch.</code></p>Populate if the related event/finding is in OCSF.
--     * Slot: type_uid Description: The unique identifier of the related OCSF event type. <p>For example: <code>100701.</code></p>Populate if the related event/finding is in OCSF.
--     * Slot: uid Description: <p>The unique identifier of the related event/finding.</p> If the related event/finding is in OCSF, then this value must be equal to <code>metadata.uid</code> in the corresponding event.
--     * Slot: OcsfFinding_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfFindingInfo_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: product_id Description: Details about the product that reported the related event/finding.
-- # Class: OcsfRemediation Description: The Remediation object describes the recommended remediation steps to address identified issue(s).
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: desc Description: The description of the remediation strategy.
-- # Class: OcsfReporter Description: The entity from which an event or finding was reported.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: hostname Description: The hostname of the entity from which the event or finding was reported.
--     * Slot: ip Description: The IP address of the entity from which the event or finding was reported.
--     * Slot: name Description: The name of the entity from which the event or finding was reported.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the entity from which the event or finding was reported.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
--     * Slot: org_id Description: The organization properties of the entity that reported the event or finding.
-- # Class: OcsfReputation Description: The Reputation object describes the reputation/risk score of an entity (e.g. device, user, domain).
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: base_score Description: The reputation score as reported by the event source.
--     * Slot: provider Description: The provider of the reputation information.
--     * Slot: score Description: The reputation score, normalized to the caption of the score_id value. In the case of 'Other', it is defined by the event source.
--     * Slot: score_id Description: The normalized reputation score identifier.
-- # Class: OcsfRequest Description: The Request Elements object describes characteristics of an API request.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: data Description: The additional data that is associated with the api request.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique request identifier.
-- # Class: OcsfResourceDetails Description: The Resource Details object describes details about a resource (including a user) affected by or related to the activity or finding. The <code>name</code> and/or <code>uid</code> should correspond to the resource for which the details pertain. Use <code>role_id</code> to characterize the role of the resource.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: cloud_partition Description: The logical grouping or isolated segment within a cloud provider's infrastructure where the resource is located. Examples include AWS partitions (aws, aws-cn, aws-us-gov), Azure cloud environments (AzureCloud, AzureUSGovernment, AzureChinaCloud), or similar logical divisions in other cloud providers.
--     * Slot: created_time_dt Description: The time when the resource was created.
--     * Slot: criticality Description: Criticality or relative importance of this resource, normalized to the caption of <code>criticality_id</code>. In the case of Other, the value is defined by the event source.<p>Note: For versions prior to 1.9, <code>criticality_id</code> was not available and this is a source specific value.<p>
--     * Slot: criticality_id Description: The normalized identifier for the criticality or relative importance of this resource. Select the value that best reflects the operational and security impact if the resource were compromised or made unavailable. See the <code>criticality</code> sibling attribute for the corresponding human-readable label.
--     * Slot: hostname Description: The fully qualified name of the resource. If the resource is a device, this should match <code>device.hostname</code>.
--     * Slot: ip Description: The IP address of the resource, in either IPv4 or IPv6 format. If the resource is a device, this should match <code>device.ip</code>.
--     * Slot: is_backed_up Description: Indicates whether the device or resource has a backup enabled, such as an automated snapshot or a cloud backup. For example, this is indicated by the <code>cloudBackupEnabled</code> value within JAMF Pro mobile devices or the registration of an AWS ARN with the AWS Backup service.
--     * Slot: modified_time_dt Description: The time when the resource was last modified.
--     * Slot: namespace Description: The namespace is useful when similar entities exist that you need to keep separate.
--     * Slot: provider Description: The cloud service provider that hosts or manages the resource. This field is typically used when the resource is managed by a different provider than the one generating the event or finding. Examples include AWS, Azure, GCP (Google Cloud Platform), Oracle Cloud, IBM Cloud, Alibaba Cloud, or other public, private, or hybrid cloud providers.
--     * Slot: region Description: The cloud region where the resource is hosted, as defined by the cloud provider. This represents the physical or logical geographic area containing the infrastructure supporting the resource. Examples include AWS regions (us-east-1, eu-west-1), Azure regions (East US, West Europe), GCP regions (us-central1, europe-west1), or Oracle Cloud regions (us-ashburn-1, uk-london-1).
--     * Slot: role Description: The role of the resource in the context of the event or finding, normalized to the caption of the role_id value. In the case of 'Other', it is defined by the event source.
--     * Slot: role_id Description: The normalized identifier of the resource's role in the context of the event or finding.
--     * Slot: version Description: The version of the resource. For example <code>1.2.3</code>.
--     * Slot: zone Description: The availability zone within a cloud region where the resource is located. Examples include AWS availability zones (us-east-1a, us-east-1b), Azure availability zones (1, 2, 3 within a region), GCP zones (us-central1-a, us-central1-b), or Oracle Cloud availability domains (AD-1, AD-2, AD-3).
--     * Slot: created_time Description: The time when the resource was created.
--     * Slot: data Description: Additional data describing the resource.
--     * Slot: modified_time Description: The time when the resource was last modified.
--     * Slot: type Description: The resource type as defined by the event source.
--     * Slot: uid_alt Description: The alternative unique identifier of the resource.
--     * Slot: name Description: The name of the resource.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the resource.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
--     * Slot: OcsfEvidences_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfIamRole_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfApiActivity_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfCloudResourcesInventoryInfo_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfFindingEvent_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfApplicationSecurityPostureFinding_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfComplianceFinding_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfDataSecurityFinding_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfDetectionFinding_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfIamAnalysisFinding_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfSecurityFinding_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfVulnerabilityFinding_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfGroupManagement_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfRoleManagement_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfUserAccess_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfUserManagement_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: device_id Description: The device details when the resource type is a device — for example, a cloud compute instance, a laptop, or a network appliance. Provides structured device attributes (OS, type, hardware, agent) that the resource's scalar fields cannot represent — beyond what the <code>hostname</code> and <code>ip</code> attributes capture. The <code>device.name</code> and <code>device.uid</code> should match the <code>name</code> and <code>uid</code> of this objects; the <code>device.hostname</code> value should match the <code>hostname</code> attribute of this object. The top-level <code>hostname</code> and <code>ip</code> remain the primary identifiers for the device resource.
--     * Slot: group_id Description: The name of the related resource group.
--     * Slot: owner_id Description: The details of the entity that owns the resource. Usually not the same as <code>user</code> that may not own the resource. For example the owner of the directory server but not a user in the directory. This object includes properties such as the owner's name, unique identifier, type, domain, and other relevant attributes that help identify the resource owner within the environment.
--     * Slot: resource_relationship_id Description: A graph representation showing how this resource relates to and interacts with other entities in the environment. This can include parent/child relationships, dependencies, or other connections.
--     * Slot: user_id Description: The user represented by this resource — for example, a cloud IAM user, a local account, or an application identity. Provides structured user attributes (email, groups, privileges) that this object's scalar fields such as <code>name</code> cannot represent. Distinct from <code>owner</code>, which identifies the entity responsible for managing the resource. The <code>user.name</code> value should match the <code>name</code> attribute of this object, <code>user.uid</code> should match the <code>uid</code> of this object.
--     * Slot: data_classification_id Description: The Data Classification object includes information about data classification levels and data category types.
-- # Class: OcsfResponse Description: The Response Elements object describes characteristics of an API response.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: code Description: The numeric response sent to a request.
--     * Slot: data Description: The additional data that is associated with the api response.
--     * Slot: error Description: Error Code
--     * Slot: error_message Description: Error Message
--     * Slot: message Description: The response message provided by the service or API.
-- # Class: OcsfRpcInterface Description: The RPC Interface represents the remote procedure call interface used in the DCE/RPC session.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: ack_reason Description: An integer that provides a reason code or additional information about the acknowledgment result.
--     * Slot: ack_result Description: An integer that denotes the acknowledgment result of the DCE/RPC call.
--     * Slot: uuid Description: The unique identifier of the particular remote procedure or service.
--     * Slot: version Description: The version of the DCE/RPC protocol being used in the session.
-- # Class: OcsfSan Description: The Subject Alternative name (SAN) object describes a SAN secured by a digital certificate
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: name Description: Name of SAN (e.g. The actual IP Address or domain.)
--     * Slot: type Description: Type descriptor of SAN (e.g. IP Address/domain/etc.)
--     * Slot: OcsfCertificate_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfTls_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
-- # Class: OcsfSbom Description: The Software Bill of Materials object describes characteristics of a generated SBOM.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: created_time Description: The time when the SBOM was created.
--     * Slot: created_time_dt Description: The time when the SBOM was created.
--     * Slot: type Description: The type of SBOM, normalized to the caption of the <code>type_id</code> value. In the case of 'Other', it is defined by the source.
--     * Slot: type_id Description: The type of SBOM.
--     * Slot: uid Description: A unique identifier for the SBOM or the SBOM generation by a source tool, such as the SPDX <code>metadata.component.bom-ref</code>.
--     * Slot: version Description: The specification (spec) version of the particular SBOM, e.g., <code>1.6</code>.
--     * Slot: package_id Description: The software package or library that is being discovered or inventoried by an SBOM.
--     * Slot: product_id Description: Details about the upstream product that generated the SBOM e.g. <code>cdxgen</code> or <code>Syft</code>.
-- # Class: OcsfScim Description: The System for Cross-domain Identity Management (SCIM) Configuration object provides a structured set of attributes related to SCIM protocols used for identity provisioning and management across cloud-based platforms. It standardizes user and group provisioning details, enabling identity synchronization and lifecycle management with compatible Identity Providers (IdPs) and applications. SCIM is defined in RFC 7634.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: auth_protocol Description: The authorization protocol as defined by the caption of <code>auth_protocol_id</code>. In the case of <code>Other</code>, it is defined by the event source.
--     * Slot: auth_protocol_id Description: The normalized identifier of the authorization protocol used by the SCIM resource.
--     * Slot: created_time Description: When the SCIM resource was added to the service provider.
--     * Slot: created_time_dt Description: When the SCIM resource was added to the service provider.
--     * Slot: error_message Description: Message or code associated with the last encountered error.
--     * Slot: is_group_provisioning_enabled Description: Indicates whether the SCIM resource is configured to provision groups, automatically or otherwise.
--     * Slot: is_user_provisioning_enabled Description: Indicates whether the SCIM resource is configured to provision users, automatically or otherwise.
--     * Slot: last_run_time Description: Timestamp of the most recent successful synchronization.
--     * Slot: last_run_time_dt Description: Timestamp of the most recent successful synchronization.
--     * Slot: modified_time Description: The most recent time when the SCIM resource was updated at the service provider.
--     * Slot: modified_time_dt Description: The most recent time when the SCIM resource was updated at the service provider.
--     * Slot: name Description: The name of the SCIM resource.
--     * Slot: protocol_name Description: The supported protocol for the SCIM resource. E.g., <code>SAML</code>, <code>OIDC</code>, or <code>OAuth2</code>.
--     * Slot: rate_limit Description: Maximum number of requests allowed by the SCIM resource within a specified time frame to avoid throttling.
--     * Slot: scim_group_schema Description: SCIM provides a schema for representing groups, identified using the following schema URI: <code>urn:ietf:params:scim:schemas:core:2.0:Group</code> as defined in RFC 7634. This attribute will capture key-value pairs for the scheme implemented in a SCIM resource.
--     * Slot: scim_user_schema Description: SCIM provides a resource type for user resources. The core schema for user is identified using the following schema URI: <code>urn:ietf:params:scim:schemas:core:2.0:User</code> as defined in RFC 7634. This attribute will capture key-value pairs for the scheme implemented in a SCIM resource. This object is inclusive of both the basic and Enterprise User Schema Extension.
--     * Slot: state Description: The provisioning state of the SCIM resource, normalized to the caption of the <code>state_id</code> value. In the case of <code>Other</code>, it is defined by the event source.
--     * Slot: state_id Description: The normalized state ID of the SCIM resource to reflect its activation status.
--     * Slot: uid Description: A unique identifier for a SCIM resource as defined by the service provider.
--     * Slot: uid_alt Description: A String that is an identifier for the resource as defined by the provisioning client. The <code>externalId</code> may simplify identification of a resource between the provisioning client and the service provider by allowing the client to use a filter to locate the resource with an identifier from the provisioning domain, obviating the need to store a local mapping between the provisioning domain's identifier of the resource and the identifier used by the service provider.
--     * Slot: url_string Description: The primary URL for SCIM API requests.
--     * Slot: vendor_name Description: Name of the vendor or service provider implementing SCIM. E.g., <code>Okta</code>, <code>Auth0</code>, <code>Microsoft</code>.
--     * Slot: version Description: SCIM protocol version supported e.g., <code>SCIM 2.0</code>.
-- # Class: OcsfScript Description: The Script object describes a script or command that can be executed by a shell, script engine, or interpreter. Examples include Bash, JavsScript, PowerShell, Python, VBScript, etc. Note that the term <em>script</em> here denotes not only a script contained within a file but also a script or command typed interactively by a user, supplied on the command line, or provided by some other file-less mechanism.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: name Description: Unique identifier for the script or macro, independent of the containing file, used for tracking, auditing, and security analysis.
--     * Slot: parent_uid Description: This attribute relates a sub-script to a parent script having the matching <code>uid</code> attribute. In the case of PowerShell, sub-script execution can be identified by matching the activity correlation ID of the raw ETW events provided by the OS.
--     * Slot: type Description: The script type, normalized to the caption of the <code>type_id</code> value. In the case of 'Other', it is defined by the event source.
--     * Slot: type_id Description: The normalized script type ID.
--     * Slot: uid Description: Some script engines assign a unique ID to each individual execution of a given script. This attribute captures that unique ID. In the case of PowerShell, the unique ID corresponds to the <code>ScriptBlockId</code> in the raw ETW events provided by the OS.
--     * Slot: file_id Description: Present if this script is associated with a file. Not present in the case of a file-less script.
--     * Slot: script_content_id Description: The script content, normalized to UTF-8 encoding irrespective of its original encoding. When emitting this attribute, it may be appropriate to truncate large scripts. When consuming this attribute, large scripts should be anticipated.
-- # Class: OcsfSecurityState Description: The Security State object describes the security related state of a managed entity.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: state Description: The security state, normalized to the caption of the state_id value. In the case of 'Other', it is defined by the source.
--     * Slot: state_id Description: The security state of the managed entity.
--     * Slot: OcsfDeviceConfigStateChange_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
-- # Class: OcsfSensorInfo Description: The Sensor Information object describes a control point sensor that emits data, or originates detections. It can be part of an endpoint or agent running on an OS, a SaaS application, a network device, a Cyber Threat Intelligence (CTI) system, or other detection and enforcement technologies.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: sensor_layer Description: The technology or sensor layer that emitted the data, normalized to the caption of <code>sensor_layer_id</code>. When <code>sensor_layer_id</code> is <code>99</code> (Other), this attribute must contain the source-specific label.
--     * Slot: sensor_layer_id Description: The normalized identifier of the sensor layer that emitted the data. For example, use this to classify the detection surface (e.g., IPS sensor, endpoint agent, email gateway). When the type is not listed, use <code>99</code> (Other) and populate <code>sensor_layer</code> with the source-specific label.
--     * Slot: name Description: The name of the entity.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the entity.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
--     * Slot: OcsfAnalytic_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
-- # Class: OcsfService Description: The Service object describes characteristics of a service, <code> e.g. AWS EC2. </code>
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: version Description: The version of the service.
--     * Slot: name Description: The name of the service.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the service.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
-- # Class: OcsfServicePrivilegeAnalysis Description: The Service Privilege Analysis object describes privilege analysis results for a single cloud service or resource namespace. It provides metrics on privilege usage, counts by type, and mappings to potential attack techniques.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: all_privileges_unused Description: Indicates whether all privileges within this service are unused.
--     * Slot: analyzed_privileges_count Description: The total count of privileges analyzed within this service.
--     * Slot: execute_count Description: The count of execute-type privileges within this service.
--     * Slot: last_used_time Description: The most recent time any privilege in this service was used.
--     * Slot: last_used_time_dt Description: The most recent time any privilege in this service was used.
--     * Slot: name Description: The service or namespace identifier. Examples: <code>s3</code>, <code>ec2</code> (AWS); <code>Microsoft.Storage</code> (Azure); <code>storage</code> (GCP).
--     * Slot: read_count Description: The count of read-type privileges within this service.
--     * Slot: unused_privileges_count Description: The count of unused privileges within this service.
--     * Slot: write_count Description: The count of write-type privileges within this service.
--     * Slot: OcsfPermissionAnalysisResult_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
-- # Class: OcsfSession Description: The Session object describes details about an authenticated session. e.g. Session Creation Time, Session Issuer.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: count Description: The number of identical sessions spawned from the same source IP, destination IP, application, and content/threat type seen over a period of time.
--     * Slot: created_time Description: The time when the session was created.
--     * Slot: created_time_dt Description: The time when the session was created.
--     * Slot: credential_uid Description: The unique identifier of the user's credential. For example, AWS Access Key ID.
--     * Slot: expiration_reason Description: The reason which triggered the session expiration.
--     * Slot: expiration_time Description: The session expiration time.
--     * Slot: expiration_time_dt Description: The session expiration time.
--     * Slot: is_mfa Description: Indicates whether Multi Factor Authentication was used during authentication.
--     * Slot: is_remote Description: The indication of whether the session is remote.
--     * Slot: is_vpn Description: The indication of whether the session is a VPN session.
--     * Slot: issuer Description: The identifier of the session issuer.
--     * Slot: terminal Description: The Pseudo Terminal associated with the session. Ex: the tty or pts value.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the session.
--     * Slot: uid_alt Description: The alternate unique identifier of the session. e.g. AWS ARN - <code>arn:aws:sts::123344444444:assumed-role/Admin/example-session</code>.
--     * Slot: uuid Description: The universally unique identifier of the session.
-- # Class: OcsfSoftwareComponent Description: The Software Component object describes characteristics of a software component within a software package.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: author Description: The author(s) who published the software component.
--     * Slot: license Description: The software license applied to this component.
--     * Slot: name Description: The software component name.
--     * Slot: purl Description: The Package URL (PURL) to identify the software component. This is a URL that uniquely identifies the component, including the component's name, version, and type. The URL is used to locate and retrieve the component's metadata and content.
--     * Slot: related_component Description: The package URL (PURL) of the component that this software component has a relationship with.
--     * Slot: relationship Description: The relationship between two software components, normalized to the caption of the <code>relationship_id</code> value. In the case of 'Other', it is defined by the source.
--     * Slot: relationship_id Description: The normalized identifier of the relationship between two software components.
--     * Slot: type Description: The type of software component, normalized to the caption of the <code>type_id</code> value. In the case of 'Other', it is defined by the source.
--     * Slot: type_id Description: The type of software component.
--     * Slot: version Description: The software component version.
--     * Slot: OcsfSbom_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: hash_id Description: Cryptographic hash to identify the binary instance of a software component.
-- # Class: OcsfSpan Description: Represents a single unit of work or operation within a distributed trace. A span typically tracks the execution of a request across a service, capturing important details such as the operation, timestamps, and status. Spans help break down the overall trace into smaller, manageable parts, enabling detailed analysis of the performance and behavior of specific operations within the system. They are crucial for understanding latency, dependencies, and bottlenecks in complex distributed systems.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: duration Description: The total time, in milliseconds, that the span represents, calculated as the difference between start_time and end_time. It reflects the operation's performance and latency, independent of event timestamps, and accounts for normalized times used by observability tools to ensure consistency across distributed systems.
--     * Slot: end_time Description: The end timestamp of the span, essential for identifying latency and performance bottlenecks. Like the start time, this timestamp is normalized across the observability system to ensure consistency, even when events are recorded across distributed services with unsynchronized clocks. Normalized time allows for accurate duration calculations and helps observability tools track performance across services, regardless of the individual system time settings.
--     * Slot: end_time_dt Description: The end timestamp of the span, essential for identifying latency and performance bottlenecks. Like the start time, this timestamp is normalized across the observability system to ensure consistency, even when events are recorded across distributed services with unsynchronized clocks. Normalized time allows for accurate duration calculations and helps observability tools track performance across services, regardless of the individual system time settings.
--     * Slot: message Description: The message in a span (often referred to as a span event) serves as a way to record significant moments or occurrences during the span's lifecycle. This content typically manifests as log entries, annotations, or semi-structured events as a string, providing additional granularity and context about what happens at specific points during the execution of an operation.
--     * Slot: operation Description: Describes an action performed in a span, such as API requests, database queries, or computations.
--     * Slot: parent_uid Description: The ID of the parent span for this span object, establishing its relationship in the trace hierarchy.
--     * Slot: start_time Description: The start timestamp of the span, essential for identifying latency and performance bottlenecks. This timestamp is normalized across the observability system, ensuring consistency even when events occur across distributed services with potentially unsynchronized clocks. By using normalized time, observability tools can provide accurate, uniform measurements of operation performance and latency, regardless of where or when the events actually occur.
--     * Slot: start_time_dt Description: The start timestamp of the span, essential for identifying latency and performance bottlenecks. This timestamp is normalized across the observability system, ensuring consistency even when events occur across distributed services with potentially unsynchronized clocks. By using normalized time, observability tools can provide accurate, uniform measurements of operation performance and latency, regardless of where or when the events actually occur.
--     * Slot: status_code Description: Indicates the outcome of the operation in the span, such as success, failure, or error. Issues in a span typically refer to problems such as failed operations, timeouts, service unavailability, or errors in processing that can negatively impact the performance or reliability of the system. Tracking the `status_code` helps pinpoint these issues, enabling quicker identification and resolution of system inefficiencies or faults.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier for the span, used in distributed systems and microservices architectures to track and correlate requests across different components of an application. It enables tracing the flow of a request through various services.
--     * Slot: service_id Description: Identifies the service or component that generates the span, helping trace its path through the distributed system.
-- # Class: OcsfSso Description: The Single Sign-On (SSO) object provides a structure for normalizing SSO attributes, configuration, and/or settings from Identity Providers.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: auth_protocol Description: The authorization protocol as defined by the caption of <code>auth_protocol_id</code>. In the case of <code>Other</code>, it is defined by the event source.
--     * Slot: auth_protocol_id Description: The normalized identifier of the authentication protocol used by the SSO resource.
--     * Slot: created_time Description: When the SSO resource was created.
--     * Slot: created_time_dt Description: When the SSO resource was created.
--     * Slot: duration_mins Description: The duration (in minutes) for an SSO session, after which re-authentication is required.
--     * Slot: idle_timeout Description: Duration (in minutes) of allowed inactivity before Single Sign-On (SSO) session expiration.
--     * Slot: login_endpoint Description: URL for initiating an SSO login request.
--     * Slot: logout_endpoint Description: URL for initiating an SSO logout request, allowing sessions to be terminated across applications.
--     * Slot: metadata_endpoint Description: URL where metadata about the SSO configuration is available (e.g., for SAML configurations).
--     * Slot: modified_time Description: The most recent time when the SSO resource was updated.
--     * Slot: modified_time_dt Description: The most recent time when the SSO resource was updated.
--     * Slot: name Description: The name of the SSO resource.
--     * Slot: protocol_name Description: The supported protocol for the SSO resource. E.g., <code>SAML</code> or <code>OIDC</code>.
--     * Slot: uid Description: A unique identifier for a SSO resource.
--     * Slot: vendor_name Description: Name of the vendor or service provider implementing SSO. E.g., <code>Okta</code>, <code>Auth0</code>, <code>Microsoft</code>.
--     * Slot: certificate_id Description: Digital Signature associated with the SSO resource, e.g., SAML X.509 certificate details.
-- # Class: OcsfStartupItem Description: The startup item object describes an application component that has associated startup criteria and configurations.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: name Description: The unique name of the startup item.
--     * Slot: run_state Description: The run state of the startup item.
--     * Slot: run_state_id Description: The run state ID of the startup item.
--     * Slot: start_type Description: The start type of the startup item.
--     * Slot: start_type_id Description: The start type ID of the startup item.
--     * Slot: type Description: The startup item type.
--     * Slot: type_id Description: The startup item type identifier.
--     * Slot: driver_id Description: The startup item kernel driver resource.
--     * Slot: job_id Description: The startup item job resource.
--     * Slot: process_id Description: The startup item process resource.
--     * Slot: win_service_id Description: The startup item Windows service resource.
-- # Class: OcsfSubTechnique Description: The MITRE Sub-technique object describes the ATT&CK® or ATLAS™ Sub-technique ID and/or name associated to an attack.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: src_url Description: The versioned permalink of the attack sub-technique. For example: <code>https://attack.mitre.org/versions/v14/techniques/T1595/001/</code>.
--     * Slot: name Description: The name of the attack sub-technique. For example: <code>Scanning IP Blocks</code> or <code>User Execution: Unsafe ML Artifacts</code>.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the attack sub-technique. For example: <code>T1595.001</code> or <code>AML.T0011.000</code>.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
-- # Class: OcsfTable Description: The table object represents a table within a structured relational database or datastore, which contains columns and rows of data that are able to be create, updated, deleted and queried.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: created_time Description: The time when the table was known to have been created.
--     * Slot: created_time_dt Description: The time when the table was known to have been created.
--     * Slot: desc Description: The description of the table.
--     * Slot: modified_time Description: The most recent time when any changes, updates, or modifications were made within the table.
--     * Slot: modified_time_dt Description: The most recent time when any changes, updates, or modifications were made within the table.
--     * Slot: size Description: The size of the data table in bytes.
--     * Slot: name Description: The table name, ordinarily as assigned by a database administrator.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the table.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
-- # Class: OcsfTactic Description: The MITRE Tactic object describes the ATT&CK® or ATLAS™ Tactic ID and/or name that is associated to an attack.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: src_url Description: The versioned permalink of the Tactic. For example: <code>https://attack.mitre.org/versions/v14/tactics/TA0043/</code>.
--     * Slot: name Description: The Tactic name that is associated with the attack technique. For example: <code>Reconnaissance</code> or <code>ML Model Access</code>.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The Tactic ID that is associated with the attack technique. For example: <code>TA0043</code>, or <code>AML.TA0000</code>.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
--     * Slot: OcsfAttack_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
-- # Class: OcsfTechnique Description: The MITRE Technique object describes the ATT&CK® or ATLAS™ Technique ID and/or name associated to an attack.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: src_url Description: The versioned permalink of the attack technique. For example: <code>https://attack.mitre.org/versions/v14/techniques/T1595/</code>.
--     * Slot: name Description: The name of the attack technique. For example: <code>Active Scanning</code> or <code>AI Model Inference API Access</code>.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the attack technique. For example: <code>T1595</code> or <code>AML.T0040</code>.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
-- # Class: OcsfThreatActor Description: Threat actor is responsible for the observed malicious activity.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: name Description: The name of the threat actor.
--     * Slot: type Description: The classification of the threat actor based on their motivations, capabilities, or affiliations. Common types include nation-state actors, cybercriminal groups, hacktivists, or insider threats.
--     * Slot: type_id Description: The normalized datastore resource type identifier.
-- # Class: OcsfTicket Description: The Ticket object represents ticket in the customer's IT Service Management (ITSM) systems like ServiceNow, Jira, etc.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: src_url Description: The url of a ticket in the ticket system.
--     * Slot: status Description: The status of the ticket normalized to the caption of the <code>status_id</code> value. In the case of <code>99</code>, this value should as defined by the source.
--     * Slot: status_id Description: The normalized identifier for the ticket status.
--     * Slot: title Description: The title of the ticket.
--     * Slot: type Description: The linked ticket type determines whether the ticket is internal or in an external ticketing system.
--     * Slot: type_id Description: The normalized identifier for the ticket type.
--     * Slot: uid Description: Unique identifier of the ticket.
--     * Slot: OcsfIncidentProfile_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfFindingEvent_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfApplicationSecurityPostureFinding_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfComplianceFinding_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfDataSecurityFinding_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfDetectionFinding_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfIamAnalysisFinding_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfIncidentFinding_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfVulnerabilityFinding_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
-- # Class: OcsfTimespan Description: The Time Span object represents different time period durations. If a timespan is fractional, i.e. crosses one period, e.g. a week and 3 days, more than one may be populated since each member is of integral type. In that case <code>type_id</code> if present should be set to <code>Other.</code><P>A timespan may also be defined by its time interval boundaries, <code>start_time</code> and <code>end_time</code>.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: duration Description: The duration of the time span in milliseconds.
--     * Slot: duration_days Description: The duration of the time span in days.
--     * Slot: duration_hours Description: The duration of the time span in hours.
--     * Slot: duration_mins Description: The duration of the time span in minutes.
--     * Slot: duration_months Description: The duration of the time span in months.
--     * Slot: duration_secs Description: The duration of the time span in seconds.
--     * Slot: duration_weeks Description: The duration of the time span in weeks.
--     * Slot: duration_years Description: The duration of the time span in years.
--     * Slot: end_time Description: The end time or conclusion of the timespan's interval.
--     * Slot: end_time_dt Description: The end time or conclusion of the timespan's interval.
--     * Slot: start_time Description: The start time or beginning of the timespan's interval.
--     * Slot: start_time_dt Description: The start time or beginning of the timespan's interval.
--     * Slot: type Description: The type of time span duration the object represents.
--     * Slot: type_id Description: The normalized identifier for the time span duration type.
-- # Class: OcsfTls Description: The Transport Layer Security (TLS) object describes the negotiated TLS protocol used for secure communications over an establish network connection.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: alert Description: The integer value of a TLS alert per the TLS specification.
--     * Slot: cipher Description: The negotiated cipher suite.
--     * Slot: handshake_dur Description: The amount of total time for the TLS handshake to complete after the TCP connection is established, including client-side delays, in milliseconds.
--     * Slot: key_length Description: The length of the encryption key.
--     * Slot: sni Description: The Server Name Indication (SNI) extension sent by the client.
--     * Slot: version Description: The TLS protocol version.
--     * Slot: certificate_id Description: The certificate object containing information about the digital certificate.
--     * Slot: ja3_hash_id Description: The MD5 hash of a JA3 string.
--     * Slot: ja3s_hash_id Description: The MD5 hash of a JA3S string.
-- # Class: OcsfTlsExtension Description: The TLS Extension object describes additional attributes that extend the base Transport Layer Security (TLS) object.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: data Description: The data contains information specific to the particular extension type.
--     * Slot: type Description: The TLS extension type. For example: <code>Server Name</code>.
--     * Slot: type_id Description: The TLS extension type identifier.
--     * Slot: OcsfTls_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
-- # Class: OcsfTrace Description: The trace object contains information about a distributed trace,  which is crucial for observability. Traces are made up of one or more spans, which are individual units of work in application activity. Traces track the journey of a request as it moves through various services in a system, capturing key details like timing, status, and dependencies at each step. Traces provide insights into system performance, helping to identify latency, bottlenecks, and issues in complex, distributed environments.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: duration Description: The total time, in milliseconds, that the trace covers, calculated as the difference between start_time and end_time. This duration helps assess the overall performance of a request as it travels across various services, and is essential for identifying latency and potential bottlenecks within the distributed system. The trace duration may differ from individual span durations due to the propagation and processing times of the trace as it spans multiple components.
--     * Slot: end_time Description: The end timestamp of the trace, essential for identifying latency and performance bottlenecks. Like the start time, this timestamp is normalized across the trace system to ensure consistency, even when events are recorded across distributed services with unsynchronized clocks. Normalized time allows for accurate trace duration calculations and helps observability tools track overall performance across services, regardless of the individual system time settings.
--     * Slot: end_time_dt Description: The end timestamp of the trace, essential for identifying latency and performance bottlenecks. Like the start time, this timestamp is normalized across the trace system to ensure consistency, even when events are recorded across distributed services with unsynchronized clocks. Normalized time allows for accurate trace duration calculations and helps observability tools track overall performance across services, regardless of the individual system time settings.
--     * Slot: start_time Description: The start timestamp of the trace, essential for identifying latency and performance bottlenecks. Like the end time, this timestamp is normalized across the trace system to ensure consistency, even when events are recorded across distributed services with unsynchronized clocks. Normalized time enables accurate trace duration calculations and helps observability tools track performance across services, regardless of the individual system time settings.
--     * Slot: start_time_dt Description: The start timestamp of the trace, essential for identifying latency and performance bottlenecks. Like the end time, this timestamp is normalized across the trace system to ensure consistency, even when events are recorded across distributed services with unsynchronized clocks. Normalized time enables accurate trace duration calculations and helps observability tools track performance across services, regardless of the individual system time settings.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the trace used in distributed systems and microservices architecture to track and correlate requests across various components of an application.
--     * Slot: service_id Description: Identifies the service or component generating the trace, helping to track and correlate the flow of requests through various parts of a distributed system. This information is essential for understanding the role and performance of specific services within the broader context of system operations and for diagnosing issues across different components.
--     * Slot: span_id Description: Represents a single unit of work or operation within a distributed trace. A span typically tracks the execution of a request across a service, capturing important details such as the operation, timestamps, and status. Spans help break down the overall trace into smaller, manageable parts, enabling detailed analysis of the performance and behavior of specific operations within the system. They are crucial for understanding latency, dependencies, and bottlenecks in complex distributed systems.
-- # Class: OcsfTrait Description: Describes a characteristic or feature of an entity that was observed. For example, this object can be used to represent specific characteristics derived from events or findings that can be surfaced as distinguishing traits of the entity in question.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: category Description: The high-level grouping or classification this trait belongs to.
--     * Slot: type Description: The type of the trait. For example, this can be used to indicate if the trait acts as a contributing factor (increases risk/severity) or a mitigating factor (decreases risk/severity), in the context of the related finding.
--     * Slot: name Description: The name of the trait.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the trait.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
--     * Slot: OcsfFindingInfo_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfRelatedEvent_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
-- # Class: OcsfTransformationInfo Description: The transformation_info object represents the mapping or transformation used.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: lang Description: The transformation language used to transform the data.
--     * Slot: time Description: Time of the transformation.
--     * Slot: time_dt Description: Time of the transformation.
--     * Slot: url_string Description: The Uniform Resource Locator String where the mapping or transformation exists.
--     * Slot: name Description: The name of the transformation or mapping.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the mapping or transformation.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
--     * Slot: OcsfMetadata_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: product_id Description: The product or instance used to make the transformation
-- # Class: OcsfTsig Description: The TSIG (Transaction Signature) object represents a TSIG resource record as defined in RFC 2845. TSIG uses a shared secret key and one-way hash function to authenticate DNS messages. Per RFC 2845, at most one TSIG record is permitted per DNS message.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: algorithm Description: The HMAC algorithm used to compute the MAC. For example: <code>hmac-sha256</code>.
--     * Slot: error Description: The TSIG error, normalized to the caption of the <code>error_id</code> value. In the case of 'Other', it is defined by the event source.
--     * Slot: error_id Description: The normalized TSIG-specific error code as defined in RFC 2845 and RFC 6895. This is independent of the DNS message header RCODE.
--     * Slot: key_name Description: The name of the shared secret key used to sign the DNS message. For example: <code>my-tsig-key.example.com.</code>
-- # Class: OcsfUnmannedAerialSystem Description: The Unmanned Aerial System object describes the characteristics, Position Location Information (PLI), and other metadata of Unmanned Aerial Systems (UAS) and other unmanned and drone systems used in Remote ID. Remote ID is defined in the Standard Specification for Remote ID and Tracking (ASTM Designation: F3411-22a).
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: type Description: The type of the UAS. For example, Helicopter, Gyroplane, Rocket, etc.
--     * Slot: type_id Description: The UAS type identifier.
--     * Slot: uuid Description: The Unmanned Aircraft System Traffic Management (UTM) provided universal unique ID (UUID) traceable to a non-obfuscated ID where this UTM UUID acts as a 'session id' to protect exposure of operationally sensitive information.
--     * Slot: model Description: The model name of the aircraft or unmanned system.
--     * Slot: serial_number Description: The serial number of the unmanned system. This is expressed in <code>CTA-2063-A</code> format.
--     * Slot: speed Description: Ground speed of flight. This value is provided in meters per second with a minimum resolution of 0.25 m/s. Special Values: <code>Invalid</code>, <code>No Value</code>, or <code>Unknown: 255 m/s</code>.
--     * Slot: speed_accuracy Description: Provides quality/containment on horizontal ground speed. Measured in meters/second.
--     * Slot: track_direction Description: Direction of flight expressed as a “True North-based” ground track angle. This value is provided in clockwise degrees with a minimum resolution of 1 degree. If aircraft is not moving horizontally, use the “Unknown” value
--     * Slot: uid_alt Description: A secondary identification identifier for an unmanned system. This can be a Serial Number (in <code>CTA-2063-A</code> format, the Registration ID (provided by the <code>CAA</code>, a UTM, or a unique Session ID.
--     * Slot: vertical_speed Description: Vertical speed upward relative to the WGS-84 datum, measured in meters per second. Special Values: <code>Invalid</code>, <code>No Value</code>, or <code>Unknown: 63 m/s</code>.
--     * Slot: name Description: The name of the unmanned system as reported by tracking or sensing hardware.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The primary identification identifier for an unmanned system. This can be a Serial Number (in <code>CTA-2063-A</code> format, the Registration ID (provided by the <code>CAA</code>, a UTM, or a unique Session ID.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
--     * Slot: hw_info_id Description: The endpoint hardware information.
--     * Slot: location_id Description: The detailed geographical location usually associated with an IP address.
-- # Class: OcsfUnmannedSystemOperatingArea Description: The Unmanned System Operating Area object describes details about a precise area of operations for a UAS flight or mission.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: altitude_ceiling Description: Maximum altitude (WGS-84 HAE) for a group or an Intent-Based Network Participant. Measured in meters. Special Values: <code>Invalid</code>, <code>No Value</code>, or <code>Unknown: -1000 m</code>.
--     * Slot: altitude_floor Description: Minimum altitude (WGS-84 HAE) for a group or an Intent-Based Network Participant. Measured in meters. Special Values: <code>Invalid</code>, <code>No Value</code>, or <code>Unknown: -1000 m</code>.
--     * Slot: count Description: Indicates the number of UAS in the operating area.
--     * Slot: end_time Description: The date and time at which a group or an Intent-Based Network Participant operation ends. (This field is only applicable to Network Remote ID.)
--     * Slot: end_time_dt Description: The date and time at which a group or an Intent-Based Network Participant operation ends. (This field is only applicable to Network Remote ID.)
--     * Slot: radius Description: Farthest horizontal distance from the reported location at which any UA in a group may be located (meters). Also allows defining the area where an Intent-Based Network Participant operation is taking place. Default: 0 m.
--     * Slot: start_time Description: The date and time at which a group or an Intent-Based Network Participant operation starts. (This field is only applicable to Network Remote ID.)
--     * Slot: start_time_dt Description: The date and time at which a group or an Intent-Based Network Participant operation starts. (This field is only applicable to Network Remote ID.)
--     * Slot: type Description: The type of operating area. For example, <code>Takeoff Location</code>, <code>Fixed Location</code>, <code>Dynamic Location</code>.
--     * Slot: type_id Description: The operating area type identifier.
--     * Slot: aerial_height Description: Expressed as either height above takeoff location or height above ground level (AGL) for a UAS current location. This value is provided in meters and must have a minimum resolution of 1 m. Special Values: <code>Invalid</code>, <code>No Value</code>, or <code>Unknown: -1000 m</code>.
--     * Slot: city Description: The name of the city.
--     * Slot: continent Description: The name of the continent.
--     * Slot: country Description: The ISO 3166-1 Alpha-2 country code.<p><b>Note:</b> The two letter country code should be capitalized. For example: <code>US</code> or <code>CA</code>.</p>
--     * Slot: desc Description: The description of the geographical location.
--     * Slot: geodetic_altitude Description: The aircraft distance above or below the ellipsoid as measured along a line that passes through the aircraft and is normal to the surface of the WGS-84 ellipsoid. This value is provided in meters and must have a minimum resolution of 1 m. Special Values: <code>Invalid</code>, <code>No Value</code>, or <code>Unknown: -1000 m</code>.
--     * Slot: geodetic_vertical_accuracy Description: Provides quality/containment on geodetic altitude. This is based on ADS-B Geodetic Vertical Accuracy (GVA). Measured in meters.
--     * Slot: geohash Description: <p>Geohash of the geo-coordinates (latitude and longitude).</p>Geohashing is a geocoding system used to encode geographic coordinates in decimal degrees, to a single string.
--     * Slot: horizontal_accuracy Description: Provides quality/containment on horizontal position. This is based on ADS-B NACp. Measured in meters.
--     * Slot: is_on_premises Description: Indicates whether the location is on-premises.
--     * Slot: isp Description: The name of the Internet Service Provider (ISP).
--     * Slot: lat Description: The geographical Latitude coordinate represented in Decimal Degrees (DD). For example: <code>42.361145</code>.
--     * Slot: long Description: The geographical Longitude coordinate represented in Decimal Degrees (DD). For example: <code>-71.057083</code>.
--     * Slot: postal_code Description: The postal code of the location.
--     * Slot: pressure_altitude Description: The uncorrected barometric pressure altitude (based on reference standard 29.92 inHg, 1013.25 mb) provides a reference for algorithms that utilize 'altitude deltas' between aircraft. This value is provided in meters and must have a minimum resolution of 1 m.. Special Values: <code>Invalid</code>, <code>No Value</code>, or <code>Unknown: -1000 m</code>.
--     * Slot: provider Description: The provider of the geographical location data.
--     * Slot: region Description: The alphanumeric code that identifies the principal subdivision (e.g. province or state) of the country. For example, 'CH-VD' for the Canton of Vaud, Switzerland
-- # Class: OcsfUrl Description: The Uniform Resource Locator (URL) object describes the characteristics of a URL.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: domain Description: The domain portion of the URL. For example: <code>example.com</code> in <code>https://sub.example.com</code>.
--     * Slot: hostname Description: The URL host as extracted from the URL. For example: <code>www.example.com</code> from <code>www.example.com/download/trouble</code>.
--     * Slot: path Description: The URL path as extracted from the URL. For example: <code>/download/trouble</code> from <code>www.example.com/download/trouble</code>.
--     * Slot: port Description: The URL port. For example: <code>80</code>.
--     * Slot: query_string Description: The query portion of the URL. For example: the query portion of the URL <code>http://www.example.com/search?q=bad&sort=date</code> is <code>q=bad&sort=date</code>.
--     * Slot: resource_type Description: The context in which a resource was retrieved in a web request.
--     * Slot: scheme Description: The scheme portion of the URL. For example: <code>http</code>, <code>https</code>, <code>ftp</code>, or <code>sftp</code>.
--     * Slot: subdomain Description: The subdomain portion of the URL. For example: <code>sub</code> in <code>https://sub.example.com</code> or <code>sub2.sub1</code> in <code>https://sub2.sub1.example.com</code>.
--     * Slot: url_string Description: The URL string. See RFC 1738. For example: <code>http://www.example.com/download/trouble.exe</code>. Note: The URL path should not populate the URL string.
--     * Slot: OcsfEmail_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
-- # Class: OcsfUser Description: The User object describes the characteristics of a user/person or a security principal.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: credential_uid Description: The unique identifier of the user's credential. For example, AWS Access Key ID.
--     * Slot: display_name Description: The display name of the user, as reported by the product.
--     * Slot: domain Description: The domain where the user is defined. For example: the LDAP or Active Directory domain.
--     * Slot: email_addr Description: The user's primary email address.
--     * Slot: forward_addr Description: The user's forwarding email address.
--     * Slot: full_name Description: The full name of the user, as reported by the product.
--     * Slot: has_mfa Description: The user has a multi-factor or secondary-factor device assigned.
--     * Slot: phone_number Description: The telephone number of the user.
--     * Slot: risk_level Description: The risk level, normalized to the caption of the risk_level_id value.
--     * Slot: risk_level_id Description: The normalized risk level id.
--     * Slot: risk_score Description: The risk score as reported by the event source.
--     * Slot: type Description: The type of the user. For example, System, AWS IAM User, etc.
--     * Slot: type_id Description: The account type identifier.
--     * Slot: uid_alt Description: The alternate user identifier. For example, the Active Directory user GUID or AWS user Principal ID.
--     * Slot: name Description: The username. For example, <code>janedoe1</code>.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique user identifier. For example, the Windows user SID, ActiveDirectory DN or AWS user ARN.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
--     * Slot: OcsfAccessAnalysisResult_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfQueryEvidence_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfAdminGroupQuery_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfGroupManagement_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: account_id Description: The user's account or the account associated with the user.
--     * Slot: ldap_person_id Description: The additional LDAP attributes that describe a person.
--     * Slot: org_id Description: Organization and org unit related to the user.
-- # Class: OcsfVendorAttributes Description: The Vendor Attributes object can be used to represent values of attributes populated by the Vendor/Finding Provider. It can help distinguish between the vendor-provided values and consumer-updated values, of key attributes like <code>severity_id</code>.<br>The original finding producer should not populate this object. It should be populated by consuming systems that support data mutability.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: severity Description: The finding severity, as reported by the Vendor (Finding Provider). The value should be normalized to the caption of the <code>severity_id</code> value. In the case of 'Other', it is defined by the source.
--     * Slot: severity_id Description: The finding severity ID, as reported by the Vendor (Finding Provider).
-- # Class: OcsfVulnerability Description: The vulnerability is an unintended characteristic of a computing component or system configuration that multiplies the risk of an adverse event or a loss occurring either due to accidental exposure, deliberate attack, or conflict with new system components.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: category Description: The category of a vulnerability or weakness, as reported by the source tool, such as <code>Container Security</code> or <code>Open Source Security</code>.
--     * Slot: dependency_chain Description: Information about the chain of dependencies related to the issue as reported by an Application Security or Vulnerability Management tool. E.g., <code>serverless-offline -> @serverless/utils -> memoizee -> es5-ext</code>.
--     * Slot: desc Description: The description of the vulnerability.
--     * Slot: exploit_last_seen_time Description: The time when the exploit was most recently observed.
--     * Slot: exploit_last_seen_time_dt Description: The time when the exploit was most recently observed.
--     * Slot: exploit_ref_url Description: The URL of the exploit code or Proof-of-Concept (PoC).
--     * Slot: exploit_requirement Description: The requirement description related to any constraints around exploit execution.
--     * Slot: exploit_type Description: The categorization or type of Exploit. E.g., <code>Network</code> or <code>Physical</code>.
--     * Slot: first_seen_time Description: The time when the vulnerability was first observed.
--     * Slot: first_seen_time_dt Description: The time when the vulnerability was first observed.
--     * Slot: fix_available Description: Indicates if a fix is available for the reported vulnerability.
--     * Slot: fix_coverage Description: The fix coverage, normalized to the caption of the <code>fix_coverage_id</code> value.
--     * Slot: fix_coverage_id Description: The normalized identifier for fix coverage, applicable to this vulnerability. Typically useful, when there are multiple affected packages but only a subset have available fixes.
--     * Slot: is_exploit_available Description: Indicates if an exploit or a PoC (proof-of-concept) is available for the reported vulnerability.
--     * Slot: is_fix_available Description: Indicates if a fix is available for the reported vulnerability.
--     * Slot: last_seen_time Description: The time when the vulnerability was most recently observed.
--     * Slot: last_seen_time_dt Description: The time when the vulnerability was most recently observed.
--     * Slot: severity Description: The vendor assigned severity of the vulnerability.
--     * Slot: title Description: A title or a brief phrase summarizing the discovered vulnerability.
--     * Slot: vendor_name Description: The name of the vendor that identified the vulnerability.
--     * Slot: OcsfOsint_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfApplicationSecurityPostureFinding_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfDetectionFinding_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfSecurityFinding_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfVulnerabilityFinding_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: advisory_id Description: Detail about the security advisory, that is used to publicly disclose cybersecurity vulnerabilities by a vendor.
--     * Slot: cve_id Description: Describes the Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures (CVE) details related to the vulnerability.
--     * Slot: cwe_id Description: Describes the Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE) details related to the vulnerability.
--     * Slot: remediation_id Description: The remediation recommendations on how to mitigate the identified vulnerability.
-- # Class: OcsfWebResource Description: The Web Resource object describes characteristics of a web resource that was affected by the activity/event.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: created_time_dt Description: The time when the resource was created.
--     * Slot: desc Description: Description of the web resource.
--     * Slot: modified_time_dt Description: The time when the resource was last modified.
--     * Slot: url_string Description: The URL pointing towards the source of the web resource.
--     * Slot: created_time Description: The time when the resource was created.
--     * Slot: data Description: Details of the web resource, e.g, <code>file</code> details, <code>search</code> results or application-defined resource.
--     * Slot: modified_time Description: The time when the resource was last modified.
--     * Slot: type Description: The web resource type as defined by the event source.
--     * Slot: uid_alt Description: The alternative unique identifier of the resource.
--     * Slot: name Description: The name of the web resource.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the web resource.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
--     * Slot: OcsfWebResourceAccessActivity_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: OcsfWebResourcesActivity_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: data_classification_id Description: The Data Classification object includes information about data classification levels and data category types.
-- # Class: OcsfWhois Description: The resources of a WHOIS record for a given domain. This can include domain names, IP address blocks, autonomous system information, and/or contact and registration information for a domain.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: created_time Description: When the domain was registered or WHOIS entry was created.
--     * Slot: created_time_dt Description: When the domain was registered or WHOIS entry was created.
--     * Slot: dnssec_status Description: The normalized value of dnssec_status_id.
--     * Slot: dnssec_status_id Description: Describes the normalized status of DNS Security Extensions (DNSSEC) for a domain.
--     * Slot: domain Description: The domain name corresponding to the WHOIS record.
--     * Slot: email_addr Description: The email address for the registrar's abuse contact
--     * Slot: isp Description: The name of the Internet Service Provider (ISP).
--     * Slot: isp_org Description: The organization name of the Internet Service Provider (ISP). This represents the parent organization or company that owns/operates the ISP. For example, Comcast Corporation would be the ISP org for Xfinity internet service. This attribute helps identify the ultimate provider when ISPs operate under different brand names.
--     * Slot: last_seen_time Description: When the WHOIS record was last updated or seen at.
--     * Slot: last_seen_time_dt Description: When the WHOIS record was last updated or seen at.
--     * Slot: phone_number Description: The phone number for the registrar's abuse contact
--     * Slot: registrar Description: The domain registrar.
--     * Slot: status Description: The status of a domain and its ability to be transferred, e.g., <code>clientTransferProhibited</code>.
--     * Slot: subnet Description: The IP address block (CIDR) associated with a domain.
--     * Slot: autonomous_system_id Description: The autonomous system information associated with a domain.
-- # Class: OcsfWinRegKey Description: The registry key object describes a Windows registry key.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: is_system Description: The indication of whether the object is part of the operating system.
--     * Slot: modified_time Description: The time when the registry key was last modified.
--     * Slot: modified_time_dt Description: The time when the registry key was last modified.
--     * Slot: path Description: The full path to the registry key.
--     * Slot: security_descriptor Description: The security descriptor of the registry key.
-- # Class: OcsfWinRegValue Description: The registry value object describes a Windows registry value.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: data Description: The data of the registry value. Where the value type is known, implementers should instead use a type-specific attribute, i.e. <code>reg_binary_data</code>, <code>reg_integer_data</code>, <code>reg_string_data</code>, or <code>reg_string_list_data</code>.
--     * Slot: is_default Description: The indication of whether the value is from a default value name. For example, the value name could be missing.
--     * Slot: is_system Description: The indication of whether the object is part of the operating system.
--     * Slot: modified_time Description: The time when the registry value was last modified.
--     * Slot: modified_time_dt Description: The time when the registry value was last modified.
--     * Slot: name Description: The name of the registry value.
--     * Slot: path Description: The full path to the registry key, where the value is located.
--     * Slot: reg_binary_data Description: The data of the registry value when <code>type_id</code> is <code>REG_BINARY</code> or <code>REG_NONE</code>.
--     * Slot: reg_integer_data Description: The data of the registry value when <code>type_id</code> is <code>REG_DWORD</code>, <code>REG_DWORD_BIG_ENDIAN</code>, or <code>REG_QWORD</code>.
--     * Slot: reg_string_data Description: The data of the registry value when <code>type_id</code> is <code>REG_SZ</code>, <code>REG_EXPAND_SZ</code>, or <code>REG_LINK</code>.
--     * Slot: type Description: A string representation of the value type as specified in Windows Registry Value Types.
--     * Slot: type_id Description: The value type ID.
-- # Class: OcsfWinResource Description: The Windows resource object describes a resource object managed by Windows, such as mutant or timer.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: created_time_dt Description: The time when the resource was created.
--     * Slot: details Description: The string detailing the attributes of the resource object.
--     * Slot: modified_time_dt Description: The time when the resource was last modified.
--     * Slot: svc_name Description: The Windows service acting as the object server for the resource object, such as Security or Security Account Manager.
--     * Slot: type_id Description: The normalized type identifier of the Windows resource object accessed.
--     * Slot: created_time Description: The time when the resource was created.
--     * Slot: data Description: Additional data describing the resource.
--     * Slot: modified_time Description: The time when the resource was last modified.
--     * Slot: type Description: The type of the Windows resource object.
--     * Slot: uid_alt Description: The alternative unique identifier of the resource.
--     * Slot: name Description: The name of the resource object.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The Windows provided handle identifier for the resource object
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
--     * Slot: data_classification_id Description: The Data Classification object includes information about data classification levels and data category types.
-- # Class: OcsfWinService Description: The Windows Service object describes a Windows service.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: cmd_line Description: The full command line used to launch the service.
--     * Slot: load_order_group Description: The name of the load ordering group of which this service is a member.
--     * Slot: service_category Description: The service category, normalized to the caption of the service_category_id value. In the case of 'Other', it is defined by the event source.
--     * Slot: service_category_id Description: The normalized identifier of the service category.
--     * Slot: service_error_control Description: The service error control, normalized to the caption of the <code>service_error_control_id</code> value. In the case of 'Other', it is defined by the event source.
--     * Slot: service_error_control_id Description: The normalized identifier of the service error control.
--     * Slot: service_start_name Description: For a user mode service, this attribute represents the name of the account under which the service is run. For a kernel mode driver, this attribute represents the object name used to load the driver.
--     * Slot: service_start_type Description: The service start type, normalized to the caption of the <code>service_start_type_id</code> value. In the case of 'Other', it is defined by the event source.
--     * Slot: service_start_type_id Description: The normalized identifier of the service start type.
--     * Slot: service_type Description: The service type, normalized to the caption of the service_type_id value. In the case of 'Other', it is defined by the event source.
--     * Slot: service_type_id Description: The normalized identifier of the service type.
--     * Slot: version Description: The version of the service.
--     * Slot: name Description: The unique name of the service.
--     * Slot: uid Description: The unique identifier of the service.
--     * Slot: uid_numeric Description: The <code>uid</code> attribute in numeric form where applicable.<br><strong>Note:</strong> Producers may populate <code>uid_numeric</code> only in addition to <code>uid</code> and not as an alternative to it.
--     * Slot: OcsfProcess_id Description: Autocreated FK slot
--     * Slot: hosting_process_id Description: The process that is hosting this service.
--     * Slot: service_dll_file_id Description: For a shared user mode service (<code>service_type_id</code> is 4) this is the DLL that gets loaded by the generic service host process (e.g. <code>svchost.exe</code>) to implement the service.
--     * Slot: service_file_id Description: For a user mode service (<code>service_type_id</code> 3 or 4) this is the executable program that the SCM launches as the service process.<br>For a kernel mode driver (<code>service_type_id</code> 1 or 2) this is the driver file loaded into the kernel at the request of the SCM.
-- # Class: OcsfBaseEvent Description: The base event is a generic and concrete event. It also defines a set of attributes available in most event classes. As a generic event that does not belong to any event category, it could be used to log events that are not otherwise defined by the schema.
--     * Slot: id
--     * Slot: activity_id Description: The normalized identifier of the activity that triggered the event. Each event class defines its own set of activity values. Use <code>0</code> (Unknown) when the activity cannot be determined. Use <code>99</code> (Other) when the activity does not match any defined value, in which case <code>activity_name</code> must be populated with the source-specific label.
--     * Slot: activity_name Description: The event activity name, as defined by the <code>activity_id</code>. When <code>activity_id</code> is <code>99</code> (Other), this attribute <b>must</b> contain the source-specific activity label. For all other <code>activity_id</code> values, this must match the caption defined for that <code>activity_id</code> enum value.
--     * Slot: category_name Description: The event category name, as defined by the <code>category_uid</code> value. The value must match the caption defined for the corresponding <code>category_uid</code> enum value (e.g., <code>"Findings"</code> for <code>category_uid: 2</code>).
--     * Slot: category_uid Description: The category unique identifier of the event. Each event class belongs to exactly one category. Producers and mappers must set this to the category defined by the event class being used.
--     * Slot: class_name Description: The event class name, as defined by class_uid value: <code>Base Event</code>.
--     * Slot: class_uid Description: The unique identifier of a class. A class describes the attributes available in an event. Producers and mappers must set this to the <code>uid</code> defined in the event class definition. For example, <code>Detection Finding</code> is <code>2004</code>.
--     * Slot: count Description: The number of events aggregated into this single record. Only populate for aggregate events. When set, <code>start_time</code> and <code>end_time</code> should also be provided to define the aggregation window.
--     * Slot: duration Description: The elapsed time of the aggregation window in milliseconds, from <code>start_time</code> to <code>end_time</code>. Only populate for aggregate events (<code>count</code> &gt; 1). The value should equal <code>end_time - start_time</code>.
--     * Slot: end_time Description: The time of the most recent event in an aggregate (<code>count</code> &gt; 1). Do not populate for discrete, point-in-time events - use <code>time</code> alone. Subclasses such as findings may redefine this for their own time-range semantics.
--     * Slot: end_time_dt Description: The time of the most recent event in an aggregate (<code>count</code> &gt; 1). Do not populate for discrete, point-in-time events - use <code>time</code> alone. Subclasses such as findings may redefine this for their own time-range semantics.
--     * Slot: message Description: A human-readable description of the event, as defined by the source. This should be a concise, meaningful summary suitable for display in a UI or alert notification - not a raw log line. For example: <code>"User john_doe logged in from 10.0.0.1."</code> rather than a raw syslog string.
--     * Slot: raw_data Description: The original event/finding data as received from the source, before normalization into OCSF. Populate this with the verbatim log line, JSON payload, or other native format for forensic and debugging purposes. This field is not intended for structured querying - use the normalized OCSF attributes instead.
--     * Slot: raw_data_size Description: The size of the original event data (as captured in <code>raw_data</code>) in bytes, before OCSF normalization. Useful for metering and capacity planning.
--     * Slot: severity Description: The event/finding severity label, normalized to the caption of the <code>severity_id</code> value. When <code>severity_id</code> is <code>99</code> (Other), this attribute <b>must</b> contain the source-specific severity label. For all other values, this should match the caption defined for that <code>severity_id</code> enum value (e.g., <code>"High"</code> for <code>severity_id: 4</code>).
--     * Slot: severity_id Description: <p>The normalized identifier of the event/finding severity.</p>The normalized severity is a measurement the effort and expense required to manage and resolve an event or incident. Smaller numerical values represent lower impact events, and larger numerical values represent higher impact events.
--     * Slot: start_time Description: The time of the earliest event in an aggregate (<code>count</code> &gt; 1). Do not populate for discrete, point-in-time events - use <code>time</code> alone. Subclasses such as findings may redefine this for their own time-range semantics.
--     * Slot: start_time_dt Description: The time of the earliest event in an aggregate (<code>count</code> &gt; 1). Do not populate for discrete, point-in-time events - use <code>time</code> alone. Subclasses such as findings may redefine this for their own time-range semantics.
--     * Slot: status Description: The event status label, normalized to the caption of the <code>status_id</code> value. When <code>status_id</code> is <code>99</code> (Other), this attribute <b>must</b> contain the source-specific status label. For all other values, this must match the caption defined for that <code>status_id</code> enum value (e.g., <code>"Success"</code> for <code>status_id: 1</code>).
--     * Slot: status_code Description: The source-specific status or error code as reported by the event source. For example, a Windows logon failure code (<code>0x18</code>), an HTTP response code (<code>403</code>), or an AWS API error code. This preserves the original code for detailed troubleshooting beyond what <code>status_id</code> conveys.
--     * Slot: status_detail Description: A human-readable description providing additional context about the event outcome. Use this to convey details that go beyond the normalized <code>status_id</code> and source-specific <code>status_code</code>, such as a failure reason or error message. For example: <code>"Account locked after 5 failed attempts."</code>.
--     * Slot: status_id Description: The normalized status of the event outcome. Use this family of attributes to convey the outcome of the activity described by the event. Producers should map their source outcome to <code>1</code> (Success) or <code>2</code> (Failure). Use <code>0</code> (Unknown) when the outcome cannot be determined, and <code>99</code> (Other) with a populated <code>status</code> string when the source value does not map cleanly.
--     * Slot: time Description: The primary timestamp of the event - when the activity actually occurred at the source. This does not capture when the event record was created or serialized by the source system; for event lifecycle timestamps such as ingestion and processing, use <code>metadata.logged_time</code> and <code>metadata.processed_time</code> respectively, or the equivalent attributes in the <code>metadata.loggers</code> array when recording pipeline stages. For aggregate events (<code>count</code> &gt; 1), set this to <code>start_time</code> (the earliest OCSF <code>time</code> in the aggregate) to preserve causal ordering and consistent timeline alignment. Note: finding classes redefine <code>time</code> as the finding creation time rather than the activity occurrence time. This <b>must</b> be a UTC epoch value in milliseconds (e.g., <code>1776881335332</code>). Mappers should use the most precise and authoritative timestamp available from the source.
--     * Slot: time_dt Description: The primary timestamp of the event - when the activity actually occurred at the source. This does not capture when the event record was created or serialized by the source system; for event lifecycle timestamps such as ingestion and processing, use <code>metadata.logged_time</code> and <code>metadata.processed_time</code> respectively, or the equivalent attributes in the <code>metadata.loggers</code> array when recording pipeline stages. For aggregate events (<code>count</code> &gt; 1), set this to <code>start_time</code> (the earliest OCSF <code>time</code> in the aggregate) to preserve causal ordering and consistent timeline alignment. Note: finding classes redefine <code>time</code> as the finding creation time rather than the activity occurrence time. This <b>must</b> be a UTC epoch value in milliseconds (e.g., <code>1776881335332</code>). Mappers should use the most precise and authoritative timestamp available from the source.
--     * Slot: timezone_offset Description: The number of minutes that the reported event <code>time</code> is ahead or behind UTC, in the range -1,080 to +1,080. This allows consumers to reconstruct the local time at the event source. For example, US Eastern Standard Time is <code>-300</code>. Populate this when the source provides local time zone information.
--     * Slot: type_name Description: The event/finding type name, combining the class and activity (e.g., <code>"Detection Finding: Create"</code>). The value must match the <code>class_name</code> and <code>activity_name</code> joined by <code>": "</code>.
--     * Slot: type_uid Description: The event/finding type ID. It identifies the event's semantics and structure. Producers and mappers <b>must</b> compute this as <code>class_uid * 100 + activity_id</code>. It uniquely identifies the combination of event class and activity across the entire schema. For example, <code>Detection Finding: Create</code> is <code>200401</code>.
--     * Slot: action Description: The normalized caption of <code>action_id</code>.
--     * Slot: action_id Description: The action taken by a control or other policy-based system leading to an outcome or disposition. An unknown action may still correspond to a known disposition. Refer to <code>disposition_id</code> for the outcome of the action.
--     * Slot: confidence Description: The confidence, normalized to the caption of the confidence_id value. In the case of 'Other', it is defined by the event source.
--     * Slot: confidence_id Description: The normalized confidence refers to the accuracy of the security control or alert. An analytic with a low confidence means that the alerts may be noisy or may not be malicious in nature.
--     * Slot: confidence_score Description: The confidence score as reported by the event source. If <code>confidence_id</code> is 99 'Other', it should be the same as <code>confidence</code> if both are populated.
--     * Slot: disposition Description: The disposition name, normalized to the caption of the disposition_id value. In the case of 'Other', it is defined by the event source.
--     * Slot: disposition_id Description: Describes the outcome or action taken by a security control, such as access control checks, malware detections or various types of policy violations.
--     * Slot: is_alert Description: Indicates that the event is considered to be an alertable signal. Should be set to <code>true</code> if <code>disposition_id = Alert</code> among other dispositions, and/or <code>risk_level_id</code> or <code>severity_id</code> of the event is elevated. Not all control events will be alertable, for example if <code>disposition_id = Exonerated</code> or <code>disposition_id = Allowed</code>.
--     * Slot: risk_details Description: Describes the risk associated with the finding.
--     * Slot: risk_level Description: The risk level, normalized to the caption of the risk_level_id value.
--     * Slot: risk_level_id Description: The normalized risk level id.
--     * Slot: risk_score Description: The risk score as reported by the event source.
--     * Slot: metadata_id Description: The metadata object describes the event producer, schema version, and processing information. Producers and mappers <b>must</b> populate <code>metadata.product</code> to identify the data source, and <code>metadata.version</code> to indicate the OCSF schema version used. Consumers rely on this to interpret the event correctly.
--     * Slot: raw_data_hash_id Description: A fingerprint (hash) of the <code>raw_data</code> content. Use this to verify the integrity of the original event data or to deduplicate events.
--     * Slot: unmapped_id Description: A container for source-specific attributes that do not map to any defined OCSF attribute. Use this to preserve valuable source data that would otherwise be lost during normalization. The keys and values are specific to the event source. <p><b>Note: </b>Consumers should not rely on a stable structure within this field. The preferred approach to unmapped attributes is to create a custom extension with the desired structure.</p>
--     * Slot: api_id Description: Describes details about a typical API (Application Programming Interface) call.
--     * Slot: cloud_id Description: Describes details about the Cloud environment where the event or finding was created.
--     * Slot: actor_id Description: The user, process, or service that initiated the activity on the host. For system-level events this is typically a process; for IAM events it is typically a user or role.
--     * Slot: device_id Description: The host or device where the activity was observed. Populate with details such as hostname, IP address, OS, and hardware identifiers when available from the source.
--     * Slot: firewall_rule_id Description: The firewall rule that pertains to the control that triggered the event, if applicable.
--     * Slot: malware_scan_info_id Description: Describes details about the scan job that identified malware on the target system.
--     * Slot: policy_id Description: The policy that pertains to the control that triggered the event, if applicable. For example the name of an anti-malware policy or an access control policy.
